(function(){(window.PhysicaNeetYear=window.PhysicaNeetYear||{})[2021]=[{id:"neet-2021-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Energy in SHM",q:"A body is executing simple harmonic motion with frequency 'n', the frequency of its potential energy is",o:["4n","n","2n","3n"],c:2,s:`Take x = A sin(\u03C9t + \u03C6).
Its frequency is n = \u03C9/2\u03C0.
Potential energy U = (1)/(2)kx\xB2 = (1)/(2)kA\xB2 sin\xB2(\u03C9t + \u03C6).
sin\xB2\u03B8 = (1 \u2212 cos 2\u03B8)/2, so U varies with 2\u03C9t.
So U repeats with angular frequency 2\u03C9, which is frequency 2n.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2021-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Polar molecules",q:"Polar molecules are the molecules",o:["Having a permanent electric dipole moment","Having zero dipole moment","Acquire a dipole moment only in the presence of electric field due to displacement of charges","Acquire a dipole moment only when magnetic field is absent"],c:0,s:`In a polar molecule the centre of positive charge and the centre of negative charge do not coincide.
So it has a dipole moment even with no field.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2021-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Drift velocity and resistivity",q:`Column-I gives certain physical terms associated with flow of current through a metallic conductor.
Column-II gives some mathematical relations involving electrical quantities.
Match Column-I and Column-II with appropriate relations.
Column-I: (A) Drift Velocity (B) Electrical Resistivity (C) Relaxation Period (D) Current Density
Column-II: (P) m/(ne\xB2\u03C1) (Q) nevd (R) eE/m (S) E/J`,o:["(A) - (R), (B) - (Q), (C) - (S), (D) - (P)","(A) - (R), (B) - (S), (C) - (P), (D) - (Q)","(A) - (R), (B) - (S), (C) - (Q), (D) - (P)","(A) - (R), (B) - (P), (C) - (S), (D) - (Q)"],c:1,s:`Acceleration of an electron is eE/m, so drift velocity is vd = (eE/m)\u03C4. A matches R (the acceleration eE/m).
Ohm's law in field form: E = \u03C1J, so \u03C1 = E/J. B matches S.
From \u03C1 = m/(ne\xB2\u03C4), \u03C4 = m/(ne\xB2\u03C1). C matches P.
J = I/A = nevd. D matches Q.
So the match is A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2021-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Charge sharing between conductors",q:"Two charged spherical conductors of radius R1 and R2 are connected by a wire. Then the ratio of surface charge densities of the spheres (\u03C31/\u03C32) is",o:["R\u2081\xB2/R\u2082\xB2","R\u2081/R\u2082","R\u2082/R\u2081","\u221A(R\u2081/R\u2082)"],c:2,s:`Joined by a wire, both spheres reach the same potential: V1 = V2.
V = Q/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080R), so Q1/R1 = Q2/R2.
Put Q = \u03C3\xB74\u03C0R\xB2: \u03C31\xB74\u03C0R1\xB2/R1 = \u03C32\xB74\u03C0R2\xB2/R2.
This gives \u03C31R1 = \u03C32R2.
So \u03C31/\u03C32 = R2/R1.
The smaller sphere has the larger surface charge density.`,fig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"Two spheres of radii R1 and R2 joined by a coiled conducting wire",d:[["c",65,80,55],["c",235,80,55],["c",65,80,1.5,"F"],["c",235,80,1.5,"F"],["l",65,80,10,80],["l",235,80,290,80],["t",28,98,"R\u2081"],["t",262,98,"R\u2082"],["l",120,80,129,80],["c",134,80,4.5],["c",143,80,4.5],["c",152,80,4.5],["c",161,80,4.5],["c",170,80,4.5],["l",175,80,180,80],["t",150,56,"Conducting","ms"],["t",150,68,"wire","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2021-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Energy stored in capacitor",q:"A parallel plate capacitor has a uniform electric field ' E ' in the space between the plates. If the distance between the plates is 'd' and the area of each plate is 'A', the energy stored in the capacitor is (\u03B50 = permittivity of free space)",o:["E\xB2Ad/\u03B5\u2080","(1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2","\u03B5\u2080EAd","(1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2Ad"],c:3,s:`The energy is stored in the field between the plates.
Energy per unit volume of an electric field is u = (1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2.
Volume between the plates = A \xD7 d.
U = u \xD7 volume = (1)/(2)\u03B5\u2080E\xB2 \xD7 Ad.`,fig:{w:300,h:160,alt:"Parallel plate capacitor, area A, separation d, uniform field E upward from the plus plate to the minus plate",d:[["p","M30 55 L200 55 L260 25 L90 25 Z"],["p","M30 120 L200 120 L260 90 L90 90 Z"],["t",62,50,"\u2013 \u2013 \u2013 \u2013 \u2013","s"],["t",34,134,"+ + + + + + +","s"],["l",50,112,50,66,"a"],["l",85,112,85,66,"a"],["l",120,112,120,66,"a"],["l",155,112,155,66,"a"],["t",12,92,"E"],["l",200,56,200,119,"b"],["t",208,78,"d"],["t",150,152,"Plate area = A","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2021-q006",pn:6,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Magnetic force on moving charge",q:"An infinitely long straight conductor carries a current of 5 A as shown. An electron is moving with a speed of 10\u2075 m/s parallel to the conductor. The perpendicular distance between the electron and the conductor is 20 cm at an instant. Calculate the magnitude of the force experienced by the electron at that instant.",o:["8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 N","4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 N","8\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070","4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 N"],c:0,s:`Field of a long wire at distance r: B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(2I/r).
B = (10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2 \xD7 5)/(0.2) = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T.
The electron moves parallel to the wire, so v is perpendicular to B.
F = qvB = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 N.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"Electron moving right at 10^5 m/s, 20 cm above a straight wire PQ carrying 5 A",d:[["l",30,140,270,140],["t",30,158,"P","m"],["t",270,158,"Q","m"],["t",150,158,"5 A","m"],["l",100,30,100,140],["l",100,30,230,30,"a"],["t",100,22,"Electron v = 10\u2075 m/s"],["l",135,32,135,138,"b"],["t",145,90,"20 cm"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2021-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensional formula",q:"If E and G respectively denote energy and gravitational constant, then E/G has the dimensions of",o:["[M\xB2][L\u207B\xB2][T\u207B\xB9]","[M\xB2][L\u207B\xB9][T\u2070]","[M][L\u207B\xB9][T\u207B\xB9]","[M][L\u2070][T\u2070]"],c:1,s:`[E] = [M L\xB2 T\u207B\xB2].
[G] = [M\u207B\xB9 L\xB3 T\u207B\xB2].
Divide: [M\xB9\u207B\u207D\u207B\xB9\u207E L\xB2\u207B\xB3 T\u207B\xB2\u207B\u207D\u207B\xB2\u207E] = [M\xB2 L\u207B\xB9 T\u2070].
So option (2).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2021-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Astronomical telescope",q:"A lens of large focal length and large aperture is best suited as an objective of an astronomical telescope since",o:["A large aperture contributes to the quality and visibility of the images.","A large area of the objective ensures better light gathering power.","A large aperture provides a better resolution.","All of the above"],c:3,s:`A large aperture collects more light, so faint objects and bright clear images are possible.
A larger diameter also gives better resolving power.
All three statements are true, so the answer is all of the above.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2021-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"Kinetic theory formulas",q:`Match Column - I and Column - II and choose the correct match from the given choices.
Column-I: (A) Root mean square speed of gas molecules (B) Pressure exerted by ideal gas (C) Average kinetic energy of a molecule (D) Total internal energy of 1 mole of a diatomic gas
Column-II: (P) (1)/(3)nmv\xB2 (Q) \u221A(3RT/M) (R) (5)/(2)RT (S) (3)/(2)kBT`,o:["(A) - (R), (B) - (Q), (C) - (P), (D) - (S)","(A) - (R), (B) - (P), (C) - (S), (D) - (Q)","(A) - (Q), (B) - (R), (C) - (S), (D) - (P)","(A) - (Q), (B) - (P), (C) - (S), (D) - (R)"],c:3,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M). A is Q.
Pressure of an ideal gas is P = (1)/(3)nmv\xB2 (n is molecules per volume). B is P.
Average kinetic energy per molecule is (3)/(2)kBT. C is S.
U = (f/2)RT per mole; a diatomic gas has f = 5, so U = (5)/(2)RT. D is R.
So A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2021-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Zener diode and p-n junction",q:`Consider the following statements (A) and (B) and identify the correct answer.
(A) A zener diode is connected in reverse bias, when used as a voltage regulator.
(B) The potential barrier of p-n junction lies between 0.1 V to 0.3 V.`,o:["(A) is incorrect but (B) is correct.","(A) and (B) both are correct.","(A) and (B) both are incorrect","(A) is correct and (B) is incorrect."],c:3,s:`A: in reverse bias beyond breakdown, the voltage across a zener stays nearly constant, so it regulates. A is correct.
B: the barrier is about 0.7 V for silicon (about 0.3 V for germanium), so 0.1 V to 0.3 V is not right. B is incorrect.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2021-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Dipole in non-uniform field",q:"A dipole is placed in an electric field as shown. In which direction will it move?",o:["Towards the right as its potential energy will increase.","Towards the left as its potential energy will increase.","Towards the right as its potential energy will decrease.","Towards the left as its potential energy will decrease."],c:2,s:`The dipole moment points from \u2013q to +q, so p points left while E points right. The angle is 180\xB0.
U = \u2013pE cos 180\xB0 = +pE, the highest value.
The field lines spread out to the right, so E gets weaker there.
Moving right lowers E, and so lowers U; a system moves to lower energy.
Torque is zero at 180\xB0, but there is a net force to the right.
So it moves right and U decreases.`,fig:{w:240,h:190,alt:"Dipole with +q left and -q right on the horizontal field line E, where the field lines spread out to the right",d:[["l",20,95,164,95,"da"],["l",20,95,136,11,"da"],["l",20,95,136,179,"da"],["c",66,86,1.6,"F"],["c",110,86,1.6,"F"],["l",66,86,110,86],["t",66,108,"+q","ms"],["t",110,108,"\u2013q","ms"],["t",170,99,"E"],["p","M169 87 H180 M177 85 L180 87 L177 89"]]},sfig:{w:240,h:190,alt:"Same field lines with the dipole moment p pointing left, opposite to E",d:[["l",20,95,164,95,"a"],["l",20,95,136,17,"a"],["l",20,95,136,173,"a"],["c",64,95,1.6,"F"],["c",104,95,1.6,"F"],["t",64,110,"+q","ms"],["t",104,110,"\u2013q","ms"],["l",100,84,70,84,"a"],["t",85,76,"P","m"],["t",170,99,"E"],["p","M79 63 H90 M87 61 L90 63 L87 65"],["p","M169 87 H180 M177 85 L180 87 L177 89"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2021-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Combination of lenses",q:"A convex lens 'A' of focal length 20 cm and a concave lens 'B' of focal length 5 cm are kept along the same axis with a distance 'd' between them. If a parallel beam of light falling on 'A' leaves 'B' as a parallel beam, then the distance 'd' in cm will be",o:["30","25","15","50"],c:2,s:`A parallel beam falling on A would meet at its focus, 20 cm behind A.
The rays reach B while still converging towards that point.
A concave lens gives a parallel beam only if the rays are aimed at its focus on the far side of B, 5 cm beyond B.
So the point 20 cm from A lies 5 cm beyond B: d + 5 = 20.
d = fA \u2013 fB = 20 \u2013 5 = 15 cm.`,sfig:{w:300,h:180,alt:"Parallel rays pass through convex lens A and converge towards its focus, concave lens B (distance d from A) makes them parallel again; fA and fB are marked",d:[["p","M60 22 C45 49 45 103 60 130 C75 103 75 49 60 22 Z"],["p","M120 20 C132 54 132 98 120 132 H150 C138 98 138 54 150 20 Z"],["l",10,49,32,49,"a"],["l",32,49,60,49],["l",10,103,32,103,"a"],["l",32,103,60,103],["l",60,49,135,61],["l",60,103,135,91],["l",135,61,225,76,"d"],["l",135,91,225,76,"d"],["l",135,61,262,61],["l",135,91,262,91],["l",250,61,254,61,"a"],["l",250,91,254,91,"a"],["l",225,67,225,85,"B"],["l",60,146,135,146,"b"],["l",135,146,225,146,"b"],["l",60,166,225,166,"b"],["t",60,12,"A","m"],["t",135,12,"B","m"],["t",98,140,"d","ms"],["t",180,140,"fB","ms"],["t",142,161,"fA","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2021-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Escape velocity",q:"The escape velocity from the Earth's surface is v. The escape velocity from the surface of another planet having a radius, four times that of Earth and same mass density is",o:["4v","v","2v","3v"],c:0,s:`ve = \u221A(2GM/R) with M = \u03C1(4/3)\u03C0R\xB3.
ve = \u221A(2G\xB7\u03C1(4/3)\u03C0R\xB3/R) = \u221A((8/3)G\u03C1\u03C0 R\xB2) = R\u221A((8/3)G\u03C1\u03C0).
For the same density, ve \u221D R.
R is 4 times larger, so the escape velocity is 4v.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2021-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Radioactive decay",q:"A radioactive nucleus \u1D2C_Z X undergoes spontaneous decay in the sequence \u1D2C_Z X \u2192 _{Z\u22121}B \u2192 _{Z\u22123}C \u2192 _{Z\u22122}D, where Z is the atomic number of element X. The possible decay particles in the sequence are",o:["\u03B2\u207B, \u03B1, \u03B2\u207A","\u03B1, \u03B2\u207B, \u03B2\u207A","\u03B1, \u03B2\u207A, \u03B2\u207B","\u03B2\u207A, \u03B1, \u03B2\u207B"],c:3,s:`Z \u2192 Z\u20131: atomic number falls by 1, so \u03B2\u207A.
Z\u20131 \u2192 Z\u20133: falls by 2, so \u03B1.
Z\u20133 \u2192 Z\u20132: rises by 1, so \u03B2\u207B.
Order: \u03B2\u207A, \u03B1, \u03B2\u207B.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2021-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Screw gauge",q:`A screw gauge gives the following readings when used to measure the diameter of a wire
Main scale reading : 0 mm Circular scale reading : 52 divisions
Given that 1 mm on main scale corresponds to 100 divisions on the circular scale. The diameter of the wire from the above data is`,o:["0.052 cm","0.52 cm","0.026 cm","0.26 cm"],c:0,s:`Pitch = 1 mm for 100 divisions.
Least count = 1/100 mm = 0.01 mm = 0.001 cm.
Diameter = 0 + 52 \xD7 0.001 cm = 0.052 cm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2021-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"LCR series circuit",q:"An inductor of inductance L, a capacitor of capacitance C and a resistor of resistance 'R' are connected in series to an ac source of potential difference 'V' volts as shown in figure. Potential difference across L, C and R is 40 V, 10 V and 40 V, respectively. The amplitude of current flowing through LCR series circuit is 10\u221A2 A. The impedance of the circuit is",o:["5 \u03A9","4\u221A2 \u03A9","5/\u221A2 \u03A9","4 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`VL and VC are opposite in phase, so net reactive voltage = 40 \u2013 10 = 30 V.
Source voltage V = \u221A(VR\xB2 + (VL \u2013 VC)\xB2) = \u221A(40\xB2 + 30\xB2) = 50 V (rms).
Irms = I\u2080/\u221A2 = 10\u221A2/\u221A2 = 10 A.
Z = V/I = 50/10 = 5 \u03A9.`,fig:{w:330,h:170,alt:"Series loop with an inductor, a capacitor and a resistor on the top wire and an ac source V at the bottom; voltages 40 V, 10 V and 40 V marked across them",d:[["p","M30 30 H65 C65 18 77 18 77 30 C77 18 89 18 89 30 C89 18 101 18 101 30 C101 18 113 18 113 30 H150"],["l",150,19,150,41],["l",157,19,157,41],["l",157,30,230,30],["p","M230 30 H234 L238 20 L244 40 L250 20 L256 40 L262 20 L266 30 H300"],["l",300,30,300,125],["l",30,30,30,125],["l",30,125,150,125],["l",172,125,300,125],["c",161,125,11],["p","M155 125 C158 119 160 119 161 125 C162 131 164 131 167 125"],["t",161,152,"V","m"],["l",78,86,31,86,"a"],["l",102,86,149,86,"a"],["t",90,90,"40 V","ms"],["l",172,86,158,86,"a"],["l",204,86,214,86,"a"],["t",188,90,"10 V","ms"],["l",258,86,216,86,"a"],["l",286,86,299,86,"a"],["t",272,90,"40 V","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2021-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Conservation of energy",q:"A particle is released from height S from the surface of the Earth. At a certain height its kinetic energy is three times its potential energy. The height from the surface of earth and the speed of the particle at that instant are respectively",o:["S/4, \u221A(3gS/2)","S/4, 3gS/2","S/4, \u221A(3gS)/2","S/2, \u221A(3gS)/2"],c:0,s:`Let the height be y. The particle fell S \u2013 y from rest.
Total energy: mgS = K + U, and K = 3U, so mgS = 4U = 4mgy.
So y = S/4.
K = 3U gives (1)/(2)mv\xB2 = 3mg(S/4).
v\xB2 = 3gS/2, so v = \u221A(3gS/2).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2021-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Distance in nth second",q:"A small block slides down on a smooth inclined plane, starting from rest at time t = 0. Let Sn be the distance travelled by the block in the interval t = n \u2013 1 to t = n. Then, the ratio Sn/Sn+1 is-",o:["2n/(2n \u2013 1)","(2n \u2013 1)/2n","(2n \u2013 1)/(2n + 1)","(2n + 1)/(2n \u2013 1)"],c:2,s:`Acceleration down a smooth incline is a = g sin \u03B8 (constant), starting from rest.
Distance in the nth second: Sn = u + (a/2)(2n \u2013 1) = (a/2)(2n \u2013 1).
For the next second: Sn+1 = (a/2)(2n + 1).
Divide: Sn/Sn+1 = (2n \u2013 1)/(2n + 1).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Straight Line"},{id:"neet-2021-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Half-life",q:"The half-life of a radioactive nuclide is 100 hours. The fraction of original activity that will remain after 150 hours would be -",o:["2/(3\u221A2)","1/2","1/(2\u221A2)","2/3"],c:2,s:`t/T\xBD = 150/100 = 3/2.
A/A\u2080 = (1/2)^(3/2) = 1/(2\u221A2).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2021-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Combination of capacitors",q:"The equivalent capacitance of the combination shown in the figure is",o:["3C/2","3C","2C","C/2"],c:2,s:`The middle capacitor has its two plates joined by wires to the same two end points (marked 1, 2, 3 in the solution), so both plates are at one potential.
It is short-circuited and stores no charge.
The other two capacitors C and C are in parallel between A and B.
Ceq = C + C = 2C.`,fig:{w:260,h:110,alt:"Three equal capacitors C: one in the top branch, one in the bottom branch and one vertical in the middle joining the two wires, between two terminals",d:[["l",22,50,50,50],["l",215,50,240,50],["l",50,23,50,78],["l",215,23,215,78],["l",50,23,79,23],["l",85,23,215,23],["l",50,78,79,78],["l",85,78,215,78],["l",79,16,79,30],["l",85,16,85,30],["l",79,71,79,85],["l",85,71,85,85],["l",145,23,145,44],["l",145,56,145,78],["c",145,23,1.8,"F"],["c",145,78,1.8,"F"],["l",137,47,153,47],["l",137,53,153,53],["t",82,14,"C","m"],["t",82,100,"C","m"],["t",158,54,"C"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:245,alt:"Given circuit with points 1, 2, 3 marked, then redrawn as two capacitors C in parallel between A and B",d:[["c",20,67,3],["l",23,67,55,67],["l",220,67,255,67],["c",258,67,3],["l",55,40,55,95],["l",220,40,220,95],["l",55,40,137,40],["l",143,40,220,40],["l",55,95,137,95],["l",143,95,220,95],["l",137,33,137,47],["l",143,33,143,47],["l",137,88,137,102],["l",143,88,143,102],["t",140,31,"C","m"],["t",140,113,"C","m"],["t",20,88,"A","m"],["t",266,88,"B","m"],["l",185,40,185,62],["l",185,74,185,95],["c",185,40,2,"F"],["c",185,95,2,"F"],["c",220,67,2,"F"],["l",177,65,193,65],["l",177,71,193,71],["t",190,34,"1","s"],["t",224,82,"2","s"],["t",190,108,"3","s"],["t",198,72,"C"],["c",20,187,3],["l",23,187,55,187],["l",220,187,255,187],["c",258,187,3],["l",55,160,55,215],["l",220,160,220,215],["l",55,160,137,160],["l",143,160,220,160],["l",55,215,137,215],["l",143,215,220,215],["l",137,153,137,167],["l",143,153,143,167],["l",137,208,137,222],["l",143,208,143,222],["t",140,151,"C","m"],["t",140,233,"C","m"],["t",20,206,"A","m"],["t",280,206,"B","m"],["t",226,208,"1, 2, 3","s"],["c",220,187,2,"F"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2021-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Series and parallel resistors",q:"The effective resistance of a parallel connection that consists of four wires of equal length, equal area of cross-section and same material is 0.25 \u03A9. What will be the effective resistance if they are connected in series?",o:["4 \u03A9","0.25 \u03A9","0.5 \u03A9","1 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`Same length, area and material, so each wire has the same resistance R.
Four in parallel: Rp = R/4 = 0.25, so R = 1 \u03A9.
Four in series: Rs = 4R = 4 \u03A9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2021-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Binding energy",q:"A nucleus with mass number 240 breaks into two fragments each of mass number 120, the binding energy per nucleon of unfragmented nuclei is 7.6 MeV while that of fragments is 8.5 MeV. The total gain in the Binding Energy in the process is",o:["216 MeV","0.9 MeV","9.4 MeV","804 MeV"],c:0,s:`Binding energy before = 240 \xD7 7.6 = 1824 MeV.
Binding energy after = 240 \xD7 8.5 = 2040 MeV.
Gain = 2040 \u2013 1824 = 216 MeV.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2021-q023",pn:23,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Electrons and holes mobility",q:"The electron concentration in an n-type semiconductor is the same as hole concentration in a p-type semiconductor. An external field (electric) is applied across each of them. Compare the currents in them.",o:["No current will flow in p-type, current will only flow in n-type","Current in n-type = current in p-type","Current in p-type > current in n-type","Current in n-type > current in p-type."],c:3,s:`Current I = n e A vd and vd = \u03BCE.
In n-type the carriers are electrons: In = n e A \u03BCe E.
In p-type they are holes: Ip = n e A \u03BCh E.
Same n, A, E, so In/Ip = \u03BCe/\u03BCh.
Electrons are more mobile than holes (\u03BCe > \u03BCh), so In > Ip.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2021-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photon number",q:"The number of photons per second on an average emitted by the source of monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm, when it delivers the power of 3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 watt will be (h = 6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 J s)",o:["10\xB9\u2075","10\xB9\u2078","10\xB9\u2077","10\xB9\u2076"],c:3,s:`Power = (photons per second) \xD7 energy of one photon = (n/t)(hc/\u03BB).
n/t = P\u03BB/(hc).
= (3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077)/(6.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2074 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078), taking c = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m/s.
= 1.98 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 / 1.98 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2075 = 10\xB9\u2076 per second.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2021-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Ampere's circuital law",q:"A thick current carrying cable of radius 'R' carries current 'I' uniformly distributed across its crosssection. The variation of magnetic field B(r) due to the cable with the distance 'r' from the axis of the cable is represented by",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:3,s:`Use Ampere's law on a circle of radius r.
Inside (r < R) only the current I\xB7r\xB2/R\xB2 is enclosed: B\xB72\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080Ir\xB2/R\xB2, so B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0R\xB2). B grows linearly with r.
Outside (r \u2265 R) all of I is enclosed: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r). B falls as 1/r.
So B rises in a straight line to a peak at r = R, then falls like a hyperbola. That is graph (4).`,fig:{w:300,h:240,alt:"Four graphs of B against r: (1) rising and levelling off, (2) rising ever more steeply, (3) a straight line through the origin, (4) a straight line rising to a peak then a decaying curve",d:[["l",20,85,20,13,"a"],["l",20,85,128,85,"a"],["t",28,21,"B","s"],["t",132,89,"r","s"],["p","M20 85 C26 37 50 23 108 21"],["t",74,107,"(1)","ms"],["l",165,85,165,13,"a"],["l",165,85,273,85,"a"],["t",173,21,"B","s"],["t",277,89,"r","s"],["p","M165 83 C205 81 237 71 251 23"],["t",219,107,"(2)","ms"],["l",20,205,20,133,"a"],["l",20,205,128,205,"a"],["t",28,141,"B","s"],["t",132,209,"r","s"],["l",20,205,108,143],["t",74,227,"(3)","ms"],["l",165,205,165,133,"a"],["l",165,205,273,205,"a"],["t",173,141,"B","s"],["t",277,209,"r","s"],["l",165,205,201,153],["l",201,153,201,205,"d"],["p","M201 153 C215 175 240 193 269 198"],["t",219,227,"(4)","ms"]]},sfig:{w:270,h:165,alt:"B against r: straight line from O to a peak at r = R, a dashed vertical at R, then a hyperbola falling for r greater than R",d:[["l",50,130,50,18,"a"],["l",30,130,235,130,"a"],["t",58,22,"B"],["t",240,134,"r"],["t",38,146,"O","m"],["t",125,146,"R","m"],["l",50,130,125,50],["l",125,50,125,130,"d"],["p","M125 50 C150 78 185 108 228 120"],["t",62,38,"Straight line","s"],["t",168,80,"Hyperbola","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2021-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Terminal velocity",q:"The velocity of a small ball of mass M and density d, when dropped in a container filled with glycerine becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is d/2, then the viscous force acting on the ball will be",o:["2Mg","Mg/2","Mg","(3)/(2)Mg"],c:1,s:`Constant velocity means the net force is zero.
Forces: weight Mg down, buoyancy FB up, viscous force Fv up.
Mg = FB + Fv.
Volume V = M/d. Buoyancy = (d/2)Vg = (1)/(2)(dV)g = Mg/2.
Fv = Mg \u2013 Mg/2 = Mg/2.`,sfig:{w:240,h:135,alt:"Ball moving down at constant velocity: weight Mg down, buoyant force FB and viscous force Fv up",d:[["c",90,60,28],["l",90,60,90,118,"a"],["l",90,60,90,12,"a"],["l",57,52,57,22,"a"],["t",90,130,"Mg","ms"],["t",96,8,"F"],["t",103,11,"B","s"],["t",50,15,"F"],["t",57,18,"v","s"],["l",150,52,150,76,"a"],["t",158,68,"v = constant","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2021-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Prism refraction",q:"Find the value of the angle of emergence from the prism. Refractive index of the glass is \u221A3.",o:["90\xBA","60\xBA","30\xBA","45\xBA"],c:1,s:`The ray hits the slanted face along the normal, so it goes in without bending.
The prism has angles 90\xB0, 60\xB0 and 30\xB0. Inside, the ray travels along the normal of the slanted face, which makes 30\xB0 with the vertical face.
So the angle of incidence on the vertical face is 30\xB0.
Snell's law from glass to air: \u221A3 sin 30\xB0 = 1 \xD7 sin e.
sin e = \u221A3/2, so e = 60\xB0.`,fig:{w:220,h:200,alt:"Right-angled prism with the right angle at bottom left and 60 degrees at bottom right; a ray falls on the slanted face along the normal",d:[["l",40,12,40,185],["l",40,185,140,185],["l",40,12,140,185],["p","M40 175 H50 V185"],["p","M118 185 A22 22 0 0 1 129 165.95"],["t",100,178,"60\xB0","ms"],["l",115,32,80,52,"a"],["l",80,52,67.5,59.6],["p","M63 51.8 L70.8 47.3 L75.3 55.1 L67.5 59.6"]]},sfig:{w:230,h:200,alt:"The same prism with 30 degrees at the top; the ray goes along the normal of the slanted face to point P on the vertical face, makes 30 degrees with the dashed normal there and leaves at angle e",d:[["l",75,12,75,185],["l",75,185,175,185],["l",75,12,175,185],["p","M75 175 H85 V185"],["p","M153 185 A22 22 0 0 1 164 165.95"],["t",135,178,"60\xB0","ms"],["p","M75 37 A25 25 0 0 0 87.5 45.65"],["t",79,76,"30\xB0","s"],["l",162.6,53.8,138.8,67.5,"a"],["l",138.8,67.5,115,81.3],["l",115,81.3,75,104.4],["p","M110.5 73.5 L118.3 69.0 L122.8 76.8 L115 81.3"],["l",40,104.4,110,104.4,"d"],["c",75,104.4,1.6,"F"],["p","M93 104.4 A18 18 0 0 0 90.6 95.4"],["t",99,101,"30\xB0","s"],["l",75,104.4,57,135.6],["l",57,135.6,50,147.7,"a"],["l",50,147.7,40,165],["p","M57 104.4 A18 18 0 0 0 66 120"],["t",45,120,"e","s"],["t",72,100,"P","es"],["t",165,36,"Incident","s"],["t",173,48,"ray","s"],["t",2,180,"Emergent","s"],["t",14,191,"ray","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2021-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Displacement current",q:"A capacitor of capacitance 'C', is connected across an ac source of voltage V, given by V = V\u2080 sin\u03C9t The displacement current between the plates of the capacitor, would then be given by",o:["Id = V\u2080\u03C9C sin\u03C9t","Id = V\u2080\u03C9C cos\u03C9t","Id = (V\u2080)/(\u03C9C) cos\u03C9t","Id = (V\u2080)/(\u03C9C) sin\u03C9t"],c:1,s:`Displacement current in the gap equals the conduction current charging the plates: Id = dQ/dt.
Q = CV, so Id = C dV/dt.
dV/dt = V\u2080\u03C9 cos\u03C9t.
Id = V\u2080\u03C9C cos\u03C9t.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2021-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensional analysis",q:"If force [F], acceleration [A] and time [T] are chosen as the fundamental physical quantities. Find the dimensions of energy.",o:["[F][A\u207B\xB9][T]","[F][A][T]","[F][A][T\xB2]","[F][A][T\u207B\xB9]"],c:2,s:`Let E = F\u1D43 A\u1D47 T\u1D9C.
[E] = [M L\xB2 T\u207B\xB2], [F] = [M L T\u207B\xB2], [A] = [L T\u207B\xB2].
Right side = M^a L^(a+b) T^(\u20132a\u20132b+c).
Mass: a = 1.
Length: a + b = 2, so b = 1.
Time: \u20132a \u2013 2b + c = \u20132 gives \u20134 + c = \u20132, so c = 2.
E = [F][A][T\xB2].`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2021-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Potentiometer",q:"In a potentiometer circuit a cell of EMF 1.5 V gives balance point at 36 cm length of wire. If another cell of EMF 2.5 V replaces the first cell, then at what length of the wire, the balance point occurs?",o:["62 cm","60 cm","21.6 cm","64 cm"],c:1,s:`In a potentiometer the wire has a uniform potential drop, so EMF \u221D balance length.
E1/E2 = l1/l2.
l2 = l1 \xD7 E2/E1 = 36 \xD7 2.5/1.5 = 60 cm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2021-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Spring-mass system",q:"A spring is stretched by 5 cm by a force 10 N. The time period of the oscillations when a mass of 2 kg is suspended by it is",o:["0.628 s","0.0628 s","6.28 s","3.14 s"],c:0,s:`Spring constant k = F/x = 10/0.05 = 200 N/m.
T = 2\u03C0\u221A(m/k) = 2\u03C0\u221A(2/200) = 2\u03C0\u221A(0.01) = 2\u03C0 \xD7 0.1.
T = 0.628 s.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2021-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Direction of E and B",q:"For a plane electromagnetic wave propagating in x-direction, which one of the following combination gives the correct possible directions for electric field (E) and magnetic field (B) respectively?",o:["\u2013\u0135 + k\u0302 , \u2013\u0135 + k\u0302","\u0135 + k\u0302 , \u0135 + k\u0302","\u2013\u0135 + k\u0302 , \u2013\u0135 \u2013 k\u0302","\u0135 + k\u0302 , \u2013\u0135 \u2013 k\u0302"],c:2,s:`E, B and the direction of travel are mutually perpendicular, and E \xD7 B points along the wave direction (x).
Options 1, 2 and 4 have B parallel (or anti-parallel) to E, so E \xD7 B = 0. Rejected.
Option 3: (\u2013\u0135 + k\u0302) \xD7 (\u2013\u0135 \u2013 k\u0302) = (\u0135 \xD7 k\u0302) + (k\u0302 \xD7 (\u2013\u0135)) = \xEE + \xEE = 2\xEE. It points along +x, and E\xB7B = 1 \u2013 1 = 0 so they are perpendicular.
So option 3.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2021-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A cup of coffee cools from 90\xB0C to 80\xB0C in t minutes, when the room temperature is 20\xB0C. The time taken by a similar cup of coffee to cool from 80\xB0C to 60\xB0C at a room temperature same at 20\xB0C is",o:["(5)/(13) t","(13)/(10) t","(13)/(5) t","(10)/(13) t"],c:2,s:`Use the average form of Newton's law: (T1 \u2013 T2)/\u0394t = K[(T1 + T2)/2 \u2013 Ts].
Case 1: 10/t = K(85 \u2013 20) = 65K, so K = 2/(13t).
Case 2: 20/t\u2081 = K(70 \u2013 20) = 50K = 100/(13t).
t\u2081 = 20 \xD7 13t/100 = 13t/5.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2021-q034",pn:34,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect and de Broglie wavelength",q:"An electromagnetic wave of wavelength '\u03BB' is incident on a photosensitive surface of negligible work function. If 'm' mass is of photoelectron emitted from the surface has de-Broglie wavelength \u03BBd, then",o:["\u03BB = (2h/mc) \u03BBd\xB2","\u03BB = (2m/hc) \u03BBd\xB2","\u03BBd = (2mc/h) \u03BB\xB2","\u03BB = (2mc/h) \u03BBd\xB2"],c:3,s:`Work function is zero, so all the photon energy becomes kinetic energy: hc/\u03BB = p\xB2/2m.
So p\xB2 = 2mhc/\u03BB.
de Broglie: \u03BBd = h/p, so \u03BBd\xB2 = h\xB2/p\xB2 = h\xB2\u03BB/(2mhc) = h\u03BB/(2mc).
Rearrange: \u03BB = (2mc/h) \u03BBd\xB2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2021-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Power",q:"Water falls from a height of 60 m at the rate of 15 kg/s to operate a turbine. The losses due to frictional force are 10% of the input energy. How much power is generated by the turbine? (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["7.0 kW","10.2 kW","8.1 kW","12.3 kW"],c:2,s:`Input power = (dm/dt) g h = 15 \xD7 10 \xD7 60 = 9000 W.
10% is lost, so 90% is delivered.
Output = 0.9 \xD7 9000 = 8100 W = 8.1 kW.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2021-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Merging of drops",q:"Twenty seven drops of same size are charged at 200 V each. They combine to form a bigger drop. Calculate the potential of the bigger drop.",o:["1980 V","660 V","1320 V","1520 V"],c:0,s:`Volume stays the same: 27 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3 = (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3, so R = 3r.
Charge is conserved: Q = 27q.
V = kQ/R = k\xB727q/(3r) = 9(kq/r) = 9 \xD7 V_small.
With the potential of one drop as 200 V this gives 1800 V, which is not an option.
With 220 V (the value used in the printed solution) it gives 9 \xD7 220 = 1980 V, which is option 1.
Note: Printed answer 1 (1980 V) needs a small-drop potential of 220 V; the question prints 200 V, which would give 1800 V (not an option). Printed key kept.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2021-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Lens and plane mirror",q:"A point object is placed at a distance of 60 cm from a convex lens of focal length 30 cm. If a plane mirror were put perpendicular to the principal axis of the lens and at a distance of 40 cm from it, the final image would be formed at a distance of",o:["20 cm from the plane mirror, it would be a virtual image","20 cm from the lens, it would be a real image","30 cm from the lens, it would be a real image","30 cm from the plane mirror, it would be a virtual image"],c:0,s:`First pass through the lens: 1/v \u2013 1/(\u201360) = 1/30, so 1/v = 1/30 \u2013 1/60 = 1/60, v = 60 cm. The image I1 is 60 cm from the lens.
The mirror is at 40 cm, so I1 is 20 cm behind it. The mirror makes its image 20 cm in front of the mirror, i.e. at 20 cm from the lens.
Light now returns to the lens, which sees an object 20 cm away (less than f).
1/v \u2013 1/(\u201320) = 1/30 gives 1/v = 1/30 \u2013 1/20 = \u20131/60, so v = \u201360 cm: a virtual image, 60 cm from the lens.
That is 60 \u2013 40 = 20 cm beyond the mirror, virtual.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2021-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Maximum height under gravity",q:"A particle of mass 'm' is projected with a velocity v = kVe (k < 1) from the surface of the earth. (Ve = escape velocity) The maximum height above the surface reached by the particle is",o:["Rk\xB2/(1 \u2013 k\xB2)","R(k/(1 \u2013 k))\xB2","R(k/(1 + k))\xB2","R\xB2k/(1 + k)"],c:0,s:`Energy conservation from the surface to the top: (1)/(2)mv\xB2 \u2013 GMm/R = \u2013GMm/(R + h).
So (1)/(2)v\xB2 = GM/R \u2013 GM/(R + h) = GMh/(R(R + h)).
Ve\xB2 = 2GM/R, so v\xB2 = k\xB2\xB72GM/R.
k\xB2\xB7GM/R = GMh/(R(R + h)), so k\xB2 = h/(R + h).
k\xB2R + k\xB2h = h, so h(1 \u2013 k\xB2) = Rk\xB2.
h = Rk\xB2/(1 \u2013 k\xB2).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2021-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Force on charge, vector form",q:`In the product
F = q(v \xD7 B) = qv \xD7 (B\xEE + B\u0135 + B\u2080k\u0302)
For q = 1 and v = 2\xEE + 4\u0135 + 6k\u0302 and F = 4\xEE \u2013 20\u0135 + 12k\u0302
What will be the complete expression for B ?`,o:["6\xEE + 6\u0135 \u2013 8k\u0302","\u20138\xEE \u2013 8\u0135 \u2013 6k\u0302","\u20136\xEE \u2013 6\u0135 \u2013 8k\u0302","8\xEE + 8\u0135 \u2013 6k\u0302"],c:2,s:`With B = (B, B, B\u2080), v \xD7 B = \xEE(4B\u2080 \u2013 6B) \u2013 \u0135(2B\u2080 \u2013 6B) + k\u0302(2B \u2013 4B).
k\u0302 part: \u20132B = 12, so B = \u20136.
\xEE part: 4B\u2080 \u2013 6B = 4, so 4B\u2080 + 36 = 4 and B\u2080 = \u20138.
Check \u0135 part: \u2013(2B\u2080 \u2013 6B) = \u2013(\u201316 + 36) = \u201320, which matches F.
So B = \u20136\xEE \u2013 6\u0135 \u2013 8k\u0302.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2021-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Mutual inductance",q:"Two conducting circular loops of radii R1 and R2 are placed in the same plane with their centres coinciding. If R1 > > R2, the mutual inductance M between them will be directly proportional to",o:["R\u2082\xB2/R\u2081","R\u2081/R\u2082","R\u2082/R\u2081","R\u2081\xB2/R\u2082"],c:0,s:`Pass a current i through the big loop (R1).
Field at the centre: B = \u03BC\u2080i/(2R1). Since R2 is tiny, B is nearly the same over the small loop.
Flux through the small loop: \u03C6 = B \xD7 \u03C0R2\xB2 = \u03BC\u2080i\u03C0R2\xB2/(2R1).
\u03C6 = Mi, so M = \u03BC\u2080\u03C0R2\xB2/(2R1).
M \u221D R2\xB2/R1.`,sfig:{w:190,h:190,alt:"Two concentric circular loops in one plane with centre O: outer loop of radius R1 carrying current i, inner loop of radius R2",d:[["c",95,95,85],["c",95,95,35],["l",95,95,140,23,"a"],["l",95,95,130,95,"a"],["t",74,92,"O"],["t",128,62,"R\u2081"],["t",100,113,"R\u2082"],["p","M162.1 152.5 L163.8 145 L157.3 148.9"],["t",171,153,"i"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2021-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Logic gates",q:"For the given circuit, the input digital signals are applied at the terminals A, B and C. What would be the output at the terminal y?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:2,s:`The circuit: an AND gate takes A and B. A NAND gate takes B and C. An OR gate joins the two outputs: y = A\xB7B + (B\xB7C)\u2032.
Check each interval of the input waveforms:
A and B are both 1 only in t3\u2013t4, where C = 0, so the NAND gives 1 anyway.
B and C are both 1 never (B is high only while C is low), so the NAND output is 1 in every interval.
Because the NAND output is always 1, the OR output is always 1: y = 5 V all the time.
That is the flat line at 5 V, graph (3).`,fig:{w:420,h:660,alt:"Input waveforms A, B, C against t1 to t6; a circuit with an AND gate on A and B, a NAND gate on B and C, and an OR gate giving y; and four output waveforms y labelled (1) to (4)",d:[["l",45,50,98,50],["l",98,50,98,22],["l",98,22,141,22],["l",141,22,141,50],["l",141,50,185,50],["l",185,50,185,22],["l",185,22,227,22],["l",227,22,227,50],["l",227,50,274,50],["l",274,50,274,22],["l",274,22,317,22],["l",317,22,317,50],["l",317,50,365,50],["l",45,123,141,123],["l",141,123,141,95],["l",141,95,227,95],["l",227,95,227,123],["l",227,123,365,123],["l",45,188,45,160],["l",45,160,141,160],["l",141,160,141,188],["l",141,188,227,188],["l",227,188,227,160],["l",227,160,365,160],["t",10,40,"A"],["t",41,26,"5","es"],["t",41,54,"0","es"],["t",10,113,"B"],["t",41,99,"5","es"],["t",41,127,"0","es"],["t",10,178,"C"],["t",41,164,"5","es"],["t",41,192,"0","es"],["l",98,22,98,188,"d"],["t",98,8,"t\u2081","ms"],["l",141,22,141,188,"d"],["t",141,8,"t\u2082","ms"],["l",185,22,185,188,"d"],["t",185,8,"t\u2083","ms"],["l",227,22,227,188,"d"],["t",227,8,"t\u2084","ms"],["l",274,22,274,188,"d"],["t",274,8,"t\u2085","ms"],["l",317,22,317,188,"d"],["t",317,8,"t\u2086","ms"],["c",25,245,3.5],["c",25,332,3.5],["c",25,423,3.5],["c",357,336,3.5],["t",22,235,"A","m"],["t",22,322,"B","m"],["t",22,413,"C","m"],["t",361,356,"y","m"],["l",28.5,245,115,245],["p","M115 238 H130 C151 238 155 260 155 275 C155 290 151 312 130 312 H115 Z"],["l",28.5,332,67,332],["l",67,332,67,302],["l",67,302,115,302],["l",67,332,67,388],["l",67,388,115,388],["c",159,410,3.2],["l",28.5,423,115,423],["l",155,275,217,275],["l",217,275,217,327],["l",217,327,272,327],["l",162,410,217,410],["l",217,410,217,360],["l",217,360,272,360],["p","M265 308 C285 308 303 318 315 338 C303 358 285 368 265 368 C277 353 277 323 265 308 Z"],["l",315,338,353.5,338],["t",50,485,"y"],["l",78,475,78,512],["t",78,470,"t\u2081","ms"],["l",124,475,124,512],["t",124,470,"t\u2082","ms"],["l",173,475,173,512],["t",173,470,"t\u2083","ms"],["l",219,475,219,512],["t",219,470,"t\u2084","ms"],["l",266,475,266,512],["t",266,470,"t\u2085","ms"],["l",314,475,314,512],["t",314,470,"t\u2086","ms"],["l",42,510,355,510],["t",358,514,"0 V","s"],["l",42,558,78,558],["l",78,558,78,530],["l",78,530,171,530],["l",171,530,171,558],["l",171,558,314,558],["l",314,558,314,530],["l",314,530,355,530],["t",358,534,"5 V","s"],["t",358,564,"0 V","s"],["l",42,584,355,584],["t",358,588,"5 V","s"],["l",42,606,78,606],["l",78,606,78,632],["l",78,632,171,632],["l",171,632,171,606],["l",171,606,314,606],["l",314,606,314,632],["l",314,632,355,632],["t",358,610,"5 V","s"],["t",358,636,"0 V","s"],["t",20,500,"(1)","m"],["t",20,550,"(2)","m"],["t",20,587,"(3)","m"],["t",20,620,"(4)","m"],["p","M115 372 H130 C151 372 155 393 155 408 C155 423 151 445 130 445 H115 Z"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2021-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Moment of inertia of ring",q:"From a circular ring of mass 'M' and radius 'R' an arc corresponding to a 90\xB0 sector is removed. The moment of inertia of the remaining part of the ring about an axis passing through the centre of the ring and perpendicular to the plane of the ring is 'K' times 'MR\xB2'. Then the value of 'K' is",o:["(1)/(8)","(3)/(4)","(7)/(8)","(1)/(4)"],c:1,s:`A 90\xB0 arc is one quarter of the ring, so its mass is M/4.
Mass left = M \u2013 M/4 = 3M/4.
Every bit of the ring is at distance R from the axis, so I = (mass) R\xB2.
I = (3M/4)R\xB2 = (3)/(4)MR\xB2.
K = 3/4.`,sfig:{w:310,h:125,alt:"A full ring with centre O, then the same ring with a 90 degree sector removed from its lower part, the angle at O marked",d:[["c",60,55,50],["c",60,55,1.6,"F"],["t",60,46,"O","ms"],["l",140,52,160,52],["l",140,58,160,58],["p","M157 48 L165 55 L157 62"],["p","M207.6 90.4 A50 50 0 1 1 278.4 90.4"],["l",243,55,207.6,90.4,"d"],["l",243,55,278.4,90.4,"d"],["c",243,55,1.6,"F"],["t",243,46,"O","ms"],["p","M233.1 64.9 A14 14 0 0 0 252.9 64.9"],["t",243,86,"90\xB0","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2021-q043",pn:43,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Series-parallel circuits",q:"Three resistors having resistances r1, r2 and r3 are connected as shown in the given circuit. The ratio i3/i1 of currents in terms of resistances used in the circuit is-",o:["r\u2082/(r\u2081 + r\u2083)","r\u2081/(r\u2082 + r\u2083)","r\u2082/(r\u2082 + r\u2083)","r\u2081/(r\u2081 + r\u2082)"],c:2,s:`r2 and r3 are in parallel, so they have the same voltage: i2r2 = i3r3.
Current splits as i1 = i2 + i3.
i2 = i3r3/r2, so i1 = i3(r3/r2 + 1) = i3(r2 + r3)/r2.
i3/i1 = r2/(r2 + r3).`,fig:{w:280,h:105,alt:"Terminal A, current i1 through r1, then r2 (current i2) in parallel with r3 (current i3), joining and going to terminal B",d:[["c",22,47,3],["l",25,47,51,47],["p","M51 47 H55 L57.0833 42 L61.25 52 L65.4167 42 L69.5833 52 L73.75 42 L77.9167 52 L80 47 H84"],["l",84,47,115,47],["l",115,47,130,20],["l",115,47,130,74],["l",130,20,157,20],["p","M157 20 H161 L163.083 15 L167.25 25 L171.417 15 L175.583 25 L179.75 15 L183.917 25 L186 20 H190"],["l",190,20,197,20],["l",197,20,212,47],["l",130,74,157,74],["p","M157 74 H161 L163.083 69 L167.25 79 L171.417 69 L175.583 79 L179.75 69 L183.917 79 L186 74 H190"],["l",190,74,197,74],["l",197,74,212,47],["l",212,47,255,47],["c",258,47,3],["t",14,63,"A"],["t",255,63,"B"],["l",29,40,44,40,"a"],["t",34,63,"i\u2081","s"],["t",63,34,"r\u2081","s"],["l",136,20,150,20,"a"],["t",132,7,"i\u2082","s"],["t",170,7,"r\u2082","s"],["l",136,74,150,74,"a"],["t",132,95,"i\u2083","s"],["t",170,95,"r\u2083","s"]]},sfig:{w:280,h:105,alt:"The same circuit with current arrows i1, i2, i3 on the wires",d:[["l",10,47,51,47],["p","M51 47 H55 L57.0833 42 L61.25 52 L65.4167 42 L69.5833 52 L73.75 42 L77.9167 52 L80 47 H84"],["l",84,47,115,47],["l",115,47,130,20],["l",115,47,130,74],["l",130,20,157,20],["p","M157 20 H161 L163.083 15 L167.25 25 L171.417 15 L175.583 25 L179.75 15 L183.917 25 L186 20 H190"],["l",190,20,197,20],["l",197,20,212,47],["l",130,74,157,74],["p","M157 74 H161 L163.083 69 L167.25 79 L171.417 69 L175.583 79 L179.75 69 L183.917 79 L186 74 H190"],["l",190,74,197,74],["l",197,74,212,47],["l",212,47,258,47],["l",20,47,38,47,"a"],["t",18,63,"i\u2081","s"],["t",63,34,"r\u2081","s"],["l",136,20,152,20,"a"],["t",132,10,"i\u2082","s"],["t",170,10,"r\u2082","s"],["l",136,74,152,74,"a"],["t",132,95,"i\u2083","s"],["t",170,95,"r\u2083","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2021-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Motion in a Plane",st:"Projectile motion",q:"A car starts from rest and accelerates at 5 m/s\xB2. At t = 4s, a ball is dropped out of a window by a person sitting in the car. What is the velocity and acceleration of the ball at t = 6 s? (Take g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["20\u221A2 m/s, 10 m/s\xB2","20 m/s, 5 m/s\xB2","20 m/s, 0","20\u221A2 m/s, 0"],c:0,s:`Car speed at t = 4 s: v = 5 \xD7 4 = 20 m/s. The ball leaves with this horizontal speed and zero vertical speed.
After leaving, only gravity acts (air ignored): horizontal speed stays 20 m/s, acceleration is g = 10 m/s\xB2 downward.
At t = 6 s the ball has fallen for 2 s: vy = 10 \xD7 2 = 20 m/s.
Speed = \u221A(20\xB2 + 20\xB2) = 20\u221A2 m/s.
Acceleration = 10 m/s\xB2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Plane"},{id:"neet-2021-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Transformer",q:"A step down transformer connected to an ac mains supply of 220 V is made to operate at 11 V, 44 W lamp. Ignoring power losses in the transformer, what is the current in the primary circuit?",o:["4 A","0.2 A","0.4 A","2 A"],c:1,s:`No losses: input power = output power = 44 W.
Vp Ip = 44, so Ip = 44/220 = 0.2 A.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2021-q046",pn:46,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Impulse",q:"A ball of mass 0.15 kg is dropped from a height 10 m, strikes the ground and rebounds to the same height. The magnitude of impulse imparted to the ball is (g = 10 m/s\xB2) nearly",o:["1.4 kg m/s","0 kg m/s","4.2 kg m/s","2.1 kg m/s"],c:2,s:`Speed just before and just after hitting the ground: v = \u221A(2gh) = \u221A(2 \xD7 10 \xD7 10) = 10\u221A2 \u2248 14.1 m/s (it rebounds to the same height).
The direction reverses, so change in momentum = m(v \u2013 (\u2013v)) = 2mv.
Impulse = 2 \xD7 0.15 \xD7 14.1 \u2248 4.2 kg m/s.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2021-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Resonance and half-power frequencies",q:"A series LCR circuit containing 5.0 H inductor, 80 \u03BCF capacitor and 40 \u03A9 resistor is connected to 230 V variable frequency ac source. The angular frequencies of the source at which power transferred to the circuit is half the power at the resonant angular frequency are likely to be-",o:["42 rad/s and 58 rad/s","25 rad/s and 75 rad/s","50 rad/s and 25 rad/s","46 rad/s and 54 rad/s"],c:3,s:`Resonant frequency: \u03C9\u2080 = 1/\u221A(LC) = 1/\u221A(5 \xD7 80 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 1/\u221A(4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 50 rad/s.
Power is half at the two frequencies where the current is 1/\u221A2 of its peak; for a sharp resonance these are \u03C9\u2080 \xB1 R/(2L).
R/(2L) = 40/(2 \xD7 5) = 4 rad/s.
So \u03C9 = 50 \u2013 4 = 46 rad/s and 50 + 4 = 54 rad/s.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2021-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Torque and equilibrium",q:"A uniform rod of length 200 cm and mass 500 g is balanced on a wedge placed at 40 cm mark. A mass of 2 kg is suspended from the rod at 20 cm and another unknown mass 'm' is suspended from the rod at 160 cm mark as shown in the figure. Find the value of 'm' such that the rod is in equilibrium. (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["(1)/(12) kg","(1)/(2) kg","(1)/(3) kg","(1)/(6) kg"],c:0,s:`Take torques about the wedge at 40 cm.
2 kg at 20 cm (20 cm to the left): 20 N \xD7 0.2 m = 4 N m, tends to turn one way (anticlockwise).
The rod's weight 5 N acts at its centre, 100 cm, which is 60 cm right of the wedge: 5 \xD7 0.6 = 3 N m, the other way.
Mass m at 160 cm is 120 cm to the right: 10m \xD7 1.2 = 12m N m, the other way.
Equilibrium: 4 = 3 + 12m.
m = 1/12 kg.`,fig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"Horizontal rod resting on a wedge, marks 0, 20 cm, 40 cm and 160 cm, a 2 kg mass hanging at the left and a mass m at the right",d:[["r",18,20,274,46],["t",15,10,"0","ms"],["t",42,10,"20 cm","ms"],["t",92,10,"40 cm","ms"],["t",250,10,"160 cm","ms"],["p","M107 68 L99 84 L115 84 Z"],["l",47,66,47,118],["l",259,66,259,118],["r",23,118,42,22],["r",234,118,42,22],["t",44,133,"2 kg","ms"],["t",255,133,"m","ms"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"The rod with the wedge as pivot O at the 40 cm mark, centre of mass CM at 100 cm, the 2 kg mass at 20 cm and m at 160 cm",d:[["r",18,24,254,42],["t",15,14,"0","ms"],["t",43,14,"20","ms"],["t",93,14,"40","ms"],["t",147,14,"100","ms"],["t",250,14,"160 cm","ms"],["c",146,45,2,"F"],["p","M102 68 L94 83 L110 83 Z"],["t",102,98,"O","ms"],["t",135,80,"CM","s"],["l",45,66,45,112],["l",239,66,239,112],["r",23,112,42,22],["r",218,112,40,22],["t",44,127,"2 kg","ms"],["t",238,127,"m","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2021-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Motion in a Plane",st:"Projectile motion",q:"A particle moving in a circle of radius R with a uniform speed takes a time T to complete one revolution. If this particle were projected with the same speed at an angle '\u03B8' to the horizontal, the maximum height attained by it equals 4R. The angle of projection, \u03B8, is then given by :",o:["\u03B8 = sin\u207B\xB9((2gT\xB2)/(\u03C0\xB2R))^(1/2)","\u03B8 = cos\u207B\xB9((gT\xB2)/(\u03C0\xB2R))^(1/2)","\u03B8 = cos\u207B\xB9((\u03C0\xB2R)/(gT\xB2))^(1/2)","\u03B8 = sin\u207B\xB9((\u03C0\xB2R)/(gT\xB2))^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`Speed on the circle: U = 2\u03C0R/T.
Maximum height of a projectile: H = U\xB2 sin\xB2\u03B8/(2g) = 4R.
sin\xB2\u03B8 = 8gR/U\xB2 = 8gR\xB7T\xB2/(4\u03C0\xB2R\xB2) = 2gT\xB2/(\u03C0\xB2R).
\u03B8 = sin\u207B\xB9[(2gT\xB2/(\u03C0\xB2R))^(1/2)].`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Plane"},{id:"neet-2021-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Magnetic dipole moment of a coil",q:`A uniform conducting wire of length 12a and resistance 'R' is wound up as a current carrying coil in the shape of,
(i) an equilateral triangle of side 'a'.
(ii) a square of side 'a'.
The magnetic dipole moments of the coil in each case respectively are`,o:["4 Ia\xB2 and 3 Ia\xB2","\u221A3 Ia\xB2 and 3 Ia\xB2","3 Ia\xB2 and Ia\xB2","3 Ia\xB2 and 4 Ia\xB2"],c:1,s:`The same voltage across the same wire gives the same current I in both cases. Magnetic moment M = N I A.
Triangle: each turn uses 3a of wire, so N = 12a/3a = 4. Area = (\u221A3/4)a\xB2.
M\u2081 = 4 \xD7 I \xD7 (\u221A3/4)a\xB2 = \u221A3 Ia\xB2.
Square: each turn uses 4a, so N = 3. Area = a\xB2.
M\u2082 = 3 \xD7 I \xD7 a\xB2 = 3Ia\xB2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2021-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Voids in close packing",q:"Right option for the number of tetrahedral and octahedral voids in hexagonal primitive unit cell are :",o:["12, 6","8, 4","6, 12","2, 1"],c:0,s:`N close-packed atoms give N octahedral voids and 2N tetrahedral voids.
A hexagonal primitive unit cell has 6 atoms.
Octahedral voids = 6, tetrahedral voids = 12.
Order asked is tetrahedral, octahedral: 12, 6.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The Solid State"},{id:"neet-2021-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoid contraction",q:"Zr (Z = 40) and Hf (Z = 72) have similar atomic and ionic radii because of :",o:["Having similar chemical properties","Belonging to same group","Diagonal relationship","Lanthanoid contraction"],c:3,s:`Between Zr and Hf the 14 lanthanoids are filled, with poorly shielding 4f electrons.
The steady shrinking of size (lanthanoid contraction) makes the 5d element Hf almost as small as the 4d element Zr (160 pm and 159 pm).
Similar properties and same group are results, not the cause.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q053",pn:53,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Boyle's law graphs",q:"Choose the correct option for graphical representation of Boyle's law, which shows a graph of pressure vs. volume of a gas at different temperatures :",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:0,s:`Boyle's law: at fixed T, PV = nRT = constant, so P-V graph is a rectangular hyperbola.
A higher T gives a larger PV, so its curve lies farther from the axes.
So the 600 K curve is outermost and 200 K is innermost. That is graph (1).
Graph (2) has the order reversed; (3) and (4) are straight lines.`,fig:{w:400,h:285,alt:"Four pressure-volume graphs: (1) three hyperbolas with 600 K outermost then 400 K and 200 K, (2) the same with the order reversed, (3) one horizontal line for all temperatures, (4) three straight lines from the origin",d:[["l",40,105,40,13,"a"],["l",40,105,174,105,"a"],["t",6,75,"(1)","s"],["t",34,115,"O","e"],["l",240,105,240,13,"a"],["l",240,105,374,105,"a"],["t",206,75,"(2)","s"],["t",234,115,"O","e"],["l",40,250,40,158,"a"],["l",40,250,174,250,"a"],["t",6,220,"(3)","s"],["t",34,260,"O","e"],["l",240,250,240,158,"a"],["l",240,250,374,250,"a"],["t",206,220,"(4)","s"],["t",234,260,"O","e"],["p","M70 25 C72 52.3 86.2 65.5 160 69"],["t",162,72,"600 K","s"],["p","M60 25 C62 61 78 79.5 160 83"],["t",162,86,"400 K","s"],["p","M50 25 C52 69.6 69.8 93.5 160 97"],["t",162,100,"200 K","s"],["p","M270 25 C272 52.3 286.2 65.5 360 69"],["t",362,72,"200 K","s"],["p","M260 25 C262 61 278 79.5 360 83"],["t",362,86,"400 K","s"],["p","M250 25 C252 69.6 269.8 93.5 360 97"],["t",362,100,"600 K","s"],["l",40,198,160,198],["t",48,193,"(200 K, 400 K, 600 K)","s"],["l",240,250,340,168],["t",342,170,"200 K","s"],["l",240,250,340,184],["t",342,186,"400 K","s"],["l",240,250,340,200],["t",342,202,"600 K","s"],["t",174,119,"V (dm\xB3)","es"],["t",374,119,"V (dm\xB3)","es"],["t",174,264,"V (dm\xB3)","es"],["t",374,264,"V (dm\xB3)","es"],["t",40,7,"P (bar)","ms"],["t",240,7,"P (bar)","ms"],["t",40,152,"P (bar)","ms"],["t",240,152,"P (bar)","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"States of Matter"},{id:"neet-2021-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Tyndall effect",q:'The right option for the statement "Tyndall effect is exhibited by", is :',o:["Urea solution","NaCl solution","Glucose solution","Starch solution"],c:3,s:`Tyndall effect (scattering of light by particles) happens only in colloids.
Urea, NaCl and glucose solutions are true solutions with tiny particles.
Starch solution is a colloid, so it shows the effect.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Surface Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2021-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Cp and Cv relation",q:"Which one among the following is the correct option for right relationship between CP and CV for one mole of ideal gas?",o:["CV = RCP","CP + CV = R","CP \u2013 CV = R","CP = RCV"],c:2,s:`For 1 mol at constant volume: q = CV\u0394T = \u0394U.
At constant pressure: q = CP\u0394T = \u0394H.
H = U + PV = U + RT, so \u0394H = \u0394U + R\u0394T.
CP\u0394T = CV\u0394T + R\u0394T.
CP \u2013 CV = R.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2021-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Anomalous behaviour of beryllium",q:"The structures of beryllium chloride in solid state and vapour phase, are :",o:["Chain in both","Chain and dimer, respectively","Linear in both","Dimer and Linear, respectively"],c:1,s:`In the solid, each Be is joined to two Cl bridges on each side, giving a polymeric chain.
In the vapour phase it forms a chloro-bridged dimer.
So chain (solid) and dimer (vapour).`,sfig:{w:280,h:185,alt:"Solid BeCl2 as a chain of Be atoms joined by double chlorine bridges, and the vapour as a dimer with two bridging Cl and one terminal Cl on each Be",d:[["t",60,48,"Be","m"],["t",135,48,"Be","m"],["t",210,48,"Be","m"],["t",97,22,"Cl","m"],["t",172,22,"Cl","m"],["t",97,74,"Cl","m"],["t",172,74,"Cl","m"],["l",69,37,88,24],["l",106,24,126,37],["l",69,51,88,61],["l",106,61,126,51],["l",144,37,163,24],["l",181,24,201,37],["l",144,51,163,61],["l",181,61,201,51],["p","M52 37 L15.6 11.4 L12.4 16.6 Z","F"],["p","M52 51 L12.4 71.4 L15.6 76.6 Z","F"],["l",219,37,248,21],["l",219,51,248,67],["t",256,24,"Cl","m"],["t",256,73,"Cl","m"],["t",75,144,"Be","m"],["t",145,144,"Be","m"],["t",110,114,"Cl","m"],["t",110,174,"Cl","m"],["t",18,144,"Cl","m"],["t",202,144,"Cl","m"],["l",29,140,66,140],["l",154,140,191,140],["l",83,134,102,121],["l",118,121,137,134],["l",83,146,102,159],["l",118,159,137,146]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Hydrogen",st:"Isotopes of hydrogen",q:"Tritium, a radioactive isotope of hydrogen, emits which of the following particles?",o:["Neutron (n)","Beta (\u03B2\u207B)","Alpha (\u03B1)","Gamma (\u03B3)"],c:1,s:`Hydrogen isotopes: protium (\xB9\u2081H), deuterium (\xB2\u2081H) and tritium (\xB3\u2081H).
Only tritium is radioactive.
It emits low-energy \u03B2\u207B particles (half-life about 12.33 years).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrogen"},{id:"neet-2021-q058",pn:58,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes",st:"Elimination and Saytzeff rule",q:"The major product formed in dehydrohalogenation reaction of 2-Bromo pentane is Pent-2-ene. This product formation is based on?",o:["Huckel's Rule","Saytzeff's Rule","Hund's Rule","Hofmann Rule"],c:1,s:`2-Bromopentane can lose H from C1 (giving pent-1-ene) or from C3 (giving pent-2-ene).
Pent-2-ene is the more substituted alkene and is formed in larger amount.
Saytzeff's rule: the preferred product is the alkene with more alkyl groups on the double-bond carbons.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes"},{id:"neet-2021-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Hybridisation of BF3",q:"BF3 is planar and electron deficient compound.Hybridization and number of electrons around the central atom, respectively are :",o:["sp\xB2 and 8","sp\xB3 and 4","sp\xB3 and 6","sp\xB2 and 6"],c:3,s:`Boron has 3 bonding pairs and no lone pair.
That is 3 \xD7 2 = 6 electrons around B (an incomplete octet).
Three electron pairs mean sp\xB2 hybridisation and a trigonal planar shape.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2021-q060",pn:60,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Grignard reagent with ketone",q:`What is the IUPAC name of the organic compound formed in the following chemical reaction?
Acetone \u2192 (i) C\u2082H\u2085MgBr, dry ether; (ii) H\u2082O, H\u207A \u2192 Product`,o:["2-methylbutan-2-ol","2-methylpropan-2-ol","pentan-2-ol","pentan-3-ol"],c:0,s:`The C\u2082H\u2085 group of the Grignard reagent attacks the carbonyl carbon of acetone (CH\u2083COCH\u2083).
This gives (CH\u2083)\u2082C(C\u2082H\u2085)OMgBr.
Water with acid converts it to a tertiary alcohol: (CH\u2083)\u2082C(OH)CH\u2082CH\u2083.
Longest chain has 4 carbons (butane), OH on C2 and CH\u2083 on C2: 2-methylbutan-2-ol.`,sfig:{w:380,h:310,alt:"Acetone reacts with ethylmagnesium bromide in dry ether to give the magnesium alkoxide, which with water and acid gives 2-methylbutan-2-ol",d:[["t",18,95,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",32,91,48,91],["t",55,95,"C","m"],["l",62,91,80,91],["t",96,95,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",55,82,55,68],["l",60,82,60,68],["t",57,64,"O","m"],["t",40,60,"\u03B4\u2212","s"],["t",42,108,"\u03B4+","s"],["l",122,92,205,92,"a"],["t",163,72,"\u2296  \u2295","ms"],["t",163,84,"(i) C\u2082H\u2085MgBr","ms"],["t",163,106,"Dry ether","ms"],["t",228,95,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",268,95,"C","m"],["t",308,95,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",244,91,260,91],["l",276,91,292,91],["t",268,50,"\u2296 \u2295","ms"],["t",268,64,"OMgBr","m"],["l",268,68,268,83],["l",268,106,268,121],["t",268,134,"C\u2082H\u2085","m"],["l",268,146,268,185,"a"],["t",276,170,"(i) H\u2082O/H\u207A","s"],["t",268,200,"OH","m"],["l",268,204,268,218],["t",228,236,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",244,232,260,232],["t",268,236,"C","m"],["l",276,232,290,232],["t",304,236,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",318,232,328,232],["t",346,236,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",268,240,268,254],["t",268,267,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",268,285,"Product","ms"],["t",268,298,"(2-methylbutan-2-ol)","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2021-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Vitamins and deficiency diseases",q:"The RBC deficiency is deficiency disease of :",o:["Vitamin B\u2082","Vitamin B\u2081\u2082","Vitamin B\u2086","Vitamin B\u2081"],c:1,s:`B\u2082 (riboflavin): cheilosis and skin burning.
B\u2081\u2082: pernicious anaemia, a shortage of red blood cells.
B\u2086 (pyridoxine): convulsions.
B\u2081 (thiamine): beri-beri.
So the RBC deficiency is due to B\u2081\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2021-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Kohlrausch law",q:"The molar conductance of NaCl, HCl and CH\u2083COONa at infinite dilution are 126.45, 426.16 and 91.0 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9 respectively. The molar conductance of CH\u2083COOH at infinite dilution is.Choose the right option for your answer.",o:["540.48 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9","201.28 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9","390.71 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9","698.28 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9"],c:2,s:`Kohlrausch law: \u039B\xB0(CH\u2083COOH) = \u03BB\xB0(CH\u2083COO\u207B) + \u03BB\xB0(H\u207A).
This equals \u039B\xB0(CH\u2083COONa) + \u039B\xB0(HCl) \u2013 \u039B\xB0(NaCl), since the Na\u207A and Cl\u207B cancel.
= 91.0 + 426.16 \u2013 126.45 = 390.71 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2021-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoids and actinoids",q:"The incorrect statement among the following is :",o:["Actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided","Actinoid contraction is greater for element to element than lanthanoid contraction","Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colorless in the solid state","Lanthanoids are good conductors of heat and electricity"],c:2,s:`Option 1 is true: actinoids are reactive when finely divided.
Option 2 is true: 5f electrons shield poorly, so contraction is larger.
Option 3 is false: many trivalent lanthanoid ions are coloured, in solid and in solution (f-f transitions).
Option 4 is true: they are metals.
So option 3 is the incorrect statement.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q064",pn:64,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Acidity of hydrogen halides",q:`Statement I : Acid strength increases in the order given as HF << HCl << HBr << HI.
Statement II : As the size of the elements F, Cl, Br, I increases down the group, the bond strength of HF, HCl, HBr and HI decreases and so the acid strength increases.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true","Both statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct but statement II is false"],c:1,s:`Going down group 17 the halogen atom gets bigger, so the H\u2013X bond gets longer and weaker.
A weaker bond lets H\u207A leave more easily, so acid strength rises: HF < HCl < HBr < HI.
Statement I is true and Statement II gives the right reason, so both are true.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"IUPAC nomenclature",q:"The correct structure of 2, 6-Dimethyl-dec-4-ene is",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:1,s:`Parent chain: dec = 10 carbons, with the double bond between C4 and C5.
Number so that the methyls are at C2 and C6.
Draw: C1 and a methyl at C2, C3, then C4=C5, then C6 with a methyl, then C7 to C10 (a butyl tail).
Only structure (2) has the double bond at C4 with methyls at C2 and C6.`,fig:{w:440,h:130,alt:"Four skeletal structures of a ten-carbon chain with two methyl branches and one double bond; they differ in the position of the double bond and of the methyl branches",d:[["l",40,70,40,50.4],["l",40,50.4,57,40.599999999999994],["l",57,40.599999999999994,74,50.4],["l",74,50.4,91,40.599999999999994],["l",91,40.599999999999994,108,50.4],["l",40,70,23,79.8],["l",91,40.599999999999994,91,21],["l",40,70,57,79.8],["l",57,79.8,74,70],["l",74,70,91,79.8],["l",91,79.8,108,70],["l",36.8,67.648,36.8,52.751999999999995],["l",150,70,150,50.4],["l",150,50.4,167,40.599999999999994],["l",167,40.599999999999994,184,50.4],["l",184,50.4,201,40.599999999999994],["l",201,40.599999999999994,218,50.4],["l",150,70,133,79.8],["l",201,40.599999999999994,201,21],["l",150,70,167,79.8],["l",167,79.8,184,70],["l",184,70,201,79.8],["l",201,79.8,218,70],["l",153.63817057265675,51.99633670766989,166.55817057265676,44.54833670766989],["l",260,50,277,40.2],["l",277,40.2,294,50],["l",294,50,311,40.2],["l",311,40.2,328,50],["l",311,40.2,311,20.599999999999998],["l",260,50,260,69.6],["l",260,69.6,277,79.4],["l",277,79.4,294,69.6],["l",294,69.6,311,79.4],["l",311,79.4,328,69.6],["l",260,50,243,40.2],["l",280.6381705726568,80.9963367076699,293.55817057265676,73.54833670766989],["l",370,50,387,40.2],["l",387,40.2,404,50],["l",404,50,421,40.2],["l",421,40.2,438,50],["l",421,40.2,421,20.599999999999998],["l",370,50,370,69.6],["l",370,69.6,387,79.4],["l",387,79.4,404,69.6],["l",404,69.6,421,79.4],["l",421,79.4,438,69.6],["l",370,69.6,353,79.4],["l",387.44182942734324,44.1483367076699,400.3618294273432,51.59633670766989],["t",6,102,"(1)","s"],["t",116,102,"(2)","s"],["t",226,102,"(3)","s"],["t",336,102,"(4)","s"]]},sfig:{w:240,h:140,alt:"Skeletal structure of 2,6-dimethyldec-4-ene with the ten carbons numbered; the double bond is between C4 and C5 and methyl groups are on C2 and C6",d:[["l",60,62,60,36.6],["l",60,36.6,82,23.900000000000006],["l",82,23.900000000000006,104,36.6],["l",104,36.6,126,23.900000000000006],["l",126,23.900000000000006,148,36.6],["l",60,62,38,74.7],["l",126,23.900000000000006,126,-1.5],["l",60,62,82,74.7],["l",82,74.7,104,62],["l",104,62,126,74.7],["l",126,74.7,148,62],["l",64.23983882403495,37.847374340847956,80.95983882403496,28.195374340847962],["t",160,40.6,"1","ms"],["t",126,39.900000000000006,"2","ms"],["t",104,53.6,"3","ms"],["t",82,15.900000000000006,"4","ms"],["t",50,36.6,"5","ms"],["t",50,62,"6","ms"],["t",82,90.7,"7","ms"],["t",104,78,"8","ms"],["t",126,90.7,"9","ms"],["t",160,66,"10","ms"],["t",120,114,"2,6-Dimethyldec-4-ene","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2021-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes",st:"C\u2013X bond enthalpy",q:"The correct sequence of bond enthalpy of 'C\u2014X' bond is :",o:["CH\u2083 \u2013 Cl > CH\u2083 \u2013 F > CH\u2083 \u2013 Br > CH\u2083 \u2013I","CH\u2083 \u2013 F < CH\u2083 \u2013 Cl < CH\u2083 \u2013 Br < CH\u2083 \u2013I","CH\u2083 \u2013 F > CH\u2083 \u2013 Cl > CH\u2083 \u2013 Br > CH\u2083 \u2013 I","CH\u2083 \u2013 F < CH\u2083 \u2013 Cl > CH\u2083 \u2013 Br > CH\u2083 \u2013 I"],c:2,s:`Halogen size increases from F to I, so the C\u2013X bond becomes longer and weaker.
Bond enthalpies (kJ mol\u207B\xB9): C\u2013F 452, C\u2013Cl 351, C\u2013Br 293, C\u2013I 234.
So F > Cl > Br > I.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes"},{id:"neet-2021-q067",pn:67,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Structure of Atom",st:"Wavelength and frequency",q:"A particular station of All India Radio, New Delhi broadcasts on a frequency of 1,368 kHz (kilohertz). The wavelength of the electromagnetic radiation emitted by the transmitter is : [speed of light c = 3.0 \xD7 10\u2078 ms\u207B\xB9]",o:["21.92 cm","219.3 m","219.2 m","2192 m"],c:1,s:`c = \u03BD\u03BB, so \u03BB = c/\u03BD.
\u03BD = 1368 kHz = 1368 \xD7 10\xB3 Hz.
\u03BB = 3.0 \xD7 10\u2078 / (1.368 \xD7 10\u2076) = 219.3 m.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Structure of Atom"},{id:"neet-2021-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"pH of salt of weak acid and weak base",q:"The pKb of dimethylamine and pKa of acetic acid are 3.27 and 4.77 respectively at T (K). The correct option for the pH of dimethylammonium acetate solution is :",o:["6.25","8.50","5.50","7.75"],c:3,s:`The salt comes from a weak acid and a weak base, so pH = 7 + (1/2)(pKa \u2013 pKb).
= 7 + (1/2)(4.77 \u2013 3.27) = 7 + 0.75.
pH = 7.75.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2021-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Solutions",st:"Osmotic pressure",q:"The following solutions were prepared by dissolving 10g of glucose (C\u2086H\u2081\u2082O\u2086) in 250 ml of water (P1),10g of urea (CH\u2084N\u2082O) in 250 ml of water (P2) and 10g of sucrose (C\u2081\u2082H\u2082\u2082O\u2081\u2081) in 250 ml of water P3).The right option for the decreasing order of osmotic pressure of these solutions is :",o:["P3 > P1 > P2","P2 > P1 > P3","P1 > P2 > P3","P2 > P3 > P1"],c:1,s:`All three solutes are non-electrolytes (i = 1) with the same mass and volume, so \u03C0 \u221D moles of solute.
Moles: urea 10/60 = 0.167; glucose 10/180 = 0.056; sucrose 10/342 = 0.029.
Higher moles give higher osmotic pressure.
Order: P2 > P1 > P3.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2021-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The Solid State",st:"Bravais lattices",q:"The correct option for the number of body centred unit cells in all 14 types of Bravais lattice unit cells is :",o:["3","7","5","2"],c:0,s:`Body-centred lattices occur in the cubic, tetragonal and orthorhombic systems.
So there are 3 body-centred unit cells.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The Solid State"},{id:"neet-2021-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Blast furnace",q:"The maximum temperature that can be achieved in blast furnace is :",o:["Upto 5000 K","Upto 1200 K","Upto 2200 K","Upto 1900 K"],c:2,s:`In the iron blast furnace the hottest zone, near the tuyeres at the bottom, reaches about 2200 K (NCERT figure shows 2170 K).
So the answer is up to 2200 K.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Potential energy profile",q:"For a reaction A \u2192 B, enthalpy of reaction is \u20134.2 kJ mol\u207B\xB9 and enthalpy of activation is 9.6 kJ mol\u207B\xB9. The correct potential energy profile for the reaction is shown in option.",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:2,s:`\u0394H = \u20134.2 kJ is negative, so the reaction is exothermic: B must lie lower than A.
Forward activation energy = 9.6 kJ, so the peak is 9.6 above A.
\u0394H = Ea(forward) \u2013 Ea(backward), so \u20134.2 = 9.6 \u2013 Ea(b), giving Ea(b) = 13.8 kJ.
So the backward barrier is higher than the forward one, and the graph must show B lower than A, with a peak 9.6 above A. Only (3) fits.`,fig:{w:470,h:270,alt:"Four potential energy against reaction progress graphs, each with a single hump between A and B: (1) B above A, (2) B slightly above A, (3) B well below A, (4) A and B at the same level",d:[["l",66,105,66,17,"a"],["l",66,105,176,105,"a"],["t",56,65,"PE","e"],["l",44,53,44,35,"a"],["t",14,65,"(1)","s"],["t",121,120,"Reaction Progress","ms"],["p","M76 92 C92 92 104 41 121 41 C138 41 140 55 154 55"],["t",92,95,"A","m"],["t",159,50,"B","m"],["l",296,105,296,17,"a"],["l",296,105,406,105,"a"],["t",286,65,"PE","e"],["l",274,53,274,35,"a"],["t",244,65,"(2)","s"],["t",351,120,"Reaction Progress","ms"],["p","M307 81 C323 81 323 38 340 38 C357 38 354 69 368 69"],["t",309,94,"A","m"],["t",372,81,"B","m"],["l",66,245,66,157,"a"],["l",66,245,176,245,"a"],["t",56,205,"PE","e"],["l",44,193,44,175,"a"],["t",14,205,"(3)","s"],["t",121,260,"Reaction Progress","ms"],["p","M70 209 C86 209 83 178 100 178 C117 178 120 234 134 234"],["t",72,222,"A","m"],["t",139,229,"B","m"],["l",296,245,296,157,"a"],["l",296,245,406,245,"a"],["t",286,205,"PE","e"],["l",274,193,274,175,"a"],["t",244,205,"(4)","s"],["t",351,260,"Reaction Progress","ms"],["p","M307 216 C323 216 324 176 341 176 C358 176 360 216 374 216"],["t",309,229,"A","m"],["t",378,228,"B","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2021-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Metamerism",q:"The compound which shows metamerism is :",o:["C\u2084H\u2081\u2080O","C\u2085H\u2081\u2082","C\u2083H\u2088O","C\u2083H\u2086O"],c:0,s:`Metamers are isomers that differ in the alkyl groups on either side of the same functional group, like ethers.
C\u2084H\u2081\u2080O can be an ether: CH\u2083CH\u2082OCH\u2082CH\u2083, CH\u2083OCH(CH\u2083)\u2082 and CH\u2083OCH\u2082CH\u2082CH\u2083 are metamers.
C\u2083H\u2088O can give only one ether (CH\u2083OC\u2082H\u2085), and C\u2085H\u2081\u2082 and C\u2083H\u2086O have no such pair.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2021-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Peroxide effect",q:`The major product of the following chemical reaction is :
(CH\u2083)\u2082CH\u2013CH=CH\u2082 + HBr \u2192(C\u2086H\u2085CO)\u2082O\u2082 ?`,o:["(CH\u2083)\u2082CBr\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2083","(CH\u2083)\u2082CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013Br","(CH\u2083)\u2082CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013O\u2013COC\u2086H\u2085","(CH\u2083)\u2082CH\u2013CHBr\u2013CH\u2083"],c:1,s:`A peroxide is present, so HBr adds by a free radical path (anti-Markovnikov).
The peroxide makes Br\u2022 radicals.
Br\u2022 adds to the terminal CH\u2082, giving the more stable secondary radical (CH\u2083)\u2082CH\u2013CH\u2022\u2013CH\u2082Br.
This radical takes H from HBr, giving (CH\u2083)\u2082CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082Br and a new Br\u2022.
So Br ends on the terminal carbon.`,fig:{w:360,h:95,alt:"Reaction: 3-methylbut-1-ene, (CH3)2CH-CH=CH2, plus HBr with benzoyl peroxide, (C6H5CO)2O2, above the arrow, gives an unknown product marked ?",d:[["t",24,22,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",24,76,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",40,28,56,44],["l",40,70,56,54],["t",72,53,"CH","m"],["l",88,49,104,49],["t",120,53,"CH","m"],["l",138,47,152,47],["l",138,51,152,51],["t",172,53,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",206,53,"+","m"],["t",234,53,"HBr","m"],["l",256,49,326,49,"a"],["t",291,40,"(C\u2086H\u2085CO)\u2082O\u2082","ms"],["t",340,53,"?","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2021-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Displacement reactions",q:"Which of the following reactions is the metal displacement reaction? Choose the right option.",o:["2Pb(NO\u2083)\u2082 \u2192(\u0394) 2PbO + 4NO\u2082 + O\u2082\u2191","2KClO\u2083 \u2192(\u0394) 2KCl + 3O\u2082","Cr\u2082O\u2083 + 2Al \u2192(\u0394) Al\u2082O\u2083 + 2Cr","Fe + 2HCl \u2192 FeCl\u2082 + H\u2082\u2191"],c:2,s:`Reactions 1 and 2 are decomposition reactions (one substance splits).
In reaction 4 a metal displaces hydrogen, not a metal.
In reaction 3 Al displaces the metal Cr from its oxide.
So (3) is a metal displacement reaction.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Redox Reactions"},{id:"neet-2021-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Chemistry in Everyday Life",st:"Analgesics",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Aspirin and Paracetamol belong to the class of narcotic analgesics.
Statement II : Morphine and Heroin are non-narcotic analgesics. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct but Statement II is false"],c:2,s:`Aspirin and paracetamol are non-narcotic analgesics, so Statement I is false.
Morphine and heroin are narcotic analgesics, so Statement II is false.
Both are false.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemistry in Everyday Life"},{id:"neet-2021-q077",pn:77,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"EDTA ligand",q:"Ethylene diaminetetraacetate (EDTA) ion is :",o:['Tridentate ligand with three "N" donor atoms','Hexadentate ligand with four "O" and two "N" donor atoms',"Unidentate ligand",'Bidentate ligand with two "N" donor atoms'],c:1,s:`EDTA\u2074\u207B has two N atoms (amine) and four carboxylate O\u207B atoms.
All six can donate a lone pair to the metal, so it is hexadentate with 4 O and 2 N donors.`,sfig:{w:300,h:235,alt:"EDTA ion bound to a metal M: two nitrogen atoms joined by a CH2-CH2 bridge, each carrying two CH2COO- arms, with arrows from the four O- atoms and the two N atoms pointing to M",d:[["t",95,64,"O\u207B","m"],["l",95,69,95,91],["t",95,104,"C","m"],["l",86,98.5,73,98.5],["l",86,101.5,73,101.5],["t",64,104,"O","m"],["t",48,92,"O\u207B","m"],["l",48,97,48,109],["t",48,122,"C","m"],["l",39,116.5,26,116.5],["l",39,119.5,26,119.5],["t",17,122,"O","m"],["l",95,109,95,130],["t",95,146,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",95,152,95,166],["t",95,178,"N","m"],["l",48,127,48,160],["t",48,174,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",63,174,86,178],["l",92,186,109,203],["l",99,168,139,84,"a"],["p","M103 61 Q125 44 138 66","a"],["p","M56 86 C78 20 140 20 138 63","a"],["t",205,64,"O\u207B","m"],["l",205,69,205,91],["t",205,104,"C","m"],["l",214,98.5,227,98.5],["l",214,101.5,227,101.5],["t",236,104,"O","m"],["t",252,92,"O\u207B","m"],["l",252,97,252,109],["t",252,122,"C","m"],["l",261,116.5,274,116.5],["l",261,119.5,274,119.5],["t",283,122,"O","m"],["l",205,109,205,130],["t",205,146,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",205,152,205,166],["t",205,178,"N","m"],["l",252,127,252,160],["t",252,174,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",237,174,214,178],["l",208,186,191,203],["l",201,168,161,84,"a"],["p","M197 61 Q175 44 162 66","a"],["p","M244 86 C222 20 160 20 162 63","a"],["t",150,72,"M","m"],["t",150,216,"CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2021-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Purification of metals",q:"Which one of the following methods can be used to obtain highly pure metal which is liquid at room temperature?",o:["Zone refining","Electrolysis","Chromatography","Distillation"],c:3,s:`Distillation purifies metals with low boiling points, like Hg (liquid at room temperature) and Zn.
The impurities are left behind as the metal vaporises and condenses again.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Amines",st:"Hinsberg test",q:"Identify the compound that will react with Hinsberg's reagent to give a solid which dissolves in alkali.",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:3,s:`Hinsberg's reagent is benzenesulphonyl chloride, C\u2086H\u2085SO\u2082Cl.
A primary amine gives RNH\u2013SO\u2082C\u2086H\u2085. This still has an acidic N\u2013H, so it dissolves in alkali.
A secondary amine gives a sulphonamide with no N\u2013H, which is insoluble in alkali.
A tertiary amine does not react. Nitroethane is not an amine.
Only (4), the primary amine CH\u2083CH\u2082NH\u2082, fits.`,fig:{w:440,h:160,alt:"Four structures: (1) N,N-diethylmethylamine, a tertiary amine, (2) nitroethane CH3CH2NO2, (3) ethylmethylamine, a secondary amine CH3CH2NHCH3, (4) ethylamine, a primary amine CH3CH2NH2",d:[["t",30,42,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",72,20,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",156,20,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",198,42,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",114,80,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",114,42,"N","m"],["t",113,28,"\xB7\xB7","s"],["l",46.830799170322955,26.183867101259406,56.94086390023736,20.88811890939948],["l",87.05913609976264,20.88811890939948,104.25585311191828,29.895923058623865],["l",123.74414688808172,29.895923058623865,140.94086390023736,20.88811890939948],["l",171.05913609976264,20.88811890939948,181.16920082967704,26.183867101259406],["l",114,47,114,56],["t",0,32,"(1)","s"],["t",270,42,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",312,20,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",358,42,"NO\u2082","m"],["t",350,28,"\xB7\xB7","s"],["l",286.83079917032296,26.183867101259406,296.94086390023733,20.88811890939948],["l",327.336281767806,20.334743454168084,340.8594497889227,26.802345551223908],["t",240,32,"(2)","s"],["t",30,132,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",72,110,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",114,132,"NH","m"],["t",113,118,"\xB7\xB7","s"],["t",160,110,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",46.830799170322955,116.18386710125941,56.94086390023736,110.88811890939948],["l",87.05913609976264,110.88811890939948,102.48419004135798,118.96790906928275],["l",125.7277448812634,119.39107853504794,142.85944978892272,111.19765444877609],["t",0,122,"(3)","s"],["t",270,132,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",312,110,"CH\u2082","m"],["t",358,132,"NH\u2082","m"],["t",357,118,"\xB7\xB7","s"],["l",286.83079917032296,116.18386710125941,296.94086390023733,110.88811890939948],["l",327.336281767806,110.33474345416808,343.5658524538297,118.09671204313592],["t",240,122,"(4)","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2021-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Covalent character of halides",q:"Among the following alkaline earth metal halides, one which is covalent and soluble in organic solvents is :",o:["Beryllium chloride","Calcium chloride","Strontium chloride","Magnesium chloride"],c:0,s:`Be\xB2\u207A is very small, so it polarises Cl\u207B strongly (Fajans' rule) and BeCl\u2082 is covalent.
Covalent compounds dissolve in organic solvents.
The chlorides of Mg, Ca and Sr are ionic.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Conformations of ethane",q:"Dihedral angle of least stable conformer of ethane is :",o:["0\xB0","120\xB0","180\xBA","60\xB0"],c:0,s:`Ethane has staggered (most stable) and eclipsed (least stable) conformers.
In the eclipsed form the H atoms on the two carbons are directly in line, so the dihedral angle is 0\xB0.
In the staggered form it is 60\xB0.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2021-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Empirical formula",q:"An organic compound contains 78% (by wt.) carbon and remaining percentage of hydrogen. The right option for the empirical formula of this compound is : [Atomic wt. of C is 12, H is 1]",o:["CH\u2084","CH","CH\u2082","CH\u2083"],c:3,s:`Take 100 g: C = 78 g, H = 22 g.
Moles C = 78/12 = 6.5. Moles H = 22/1 = 22.
Divide by 6.5: C = 1, H = 22/6.5 = 3.38.
The nearest simple whole number ratio is 1 : 3, so the empirical formula is CH\u2083.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2021-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Noble gases",q:"Noble gases are named because of their inertness towards reactivity. Identify an incorrect statement about them.",o:["Noble gases have large positive values of electron gain enthalpy","Noble gases are sparingly soluble in water","Noble gases have very high melting and boiling points","Noble gases have weak dispersion forces"],c:2,s:`Noble gases are monatomic and held only by weak dispersion forces.
So their melting and boiling points are very low, not very high.
Statement 3 is therefore incorrect. The other three statements are true.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Polymers",st:"Addition polymers",q:"Which one of the following polymers is prepared by addition polymerisation?",o:["Dacron","Teflon","Nylon-66","Novolac"],c:1,s:`Dacron (polyester), nylon-66 (polyamide) and novolac (phenol-formaldehyde) form by condensation, losing a small molecule.
Teflon forms by addition polymerisation of tetrafluoroethene, nCF\u2082=CF\u2082 \u2192 (\u2013CF\u2082\u2013CF\u2082\u2013)\u2099, using a catalyst and high pressure.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Polymers"},{id:"neet-2021-q085",pn:85,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Shapes of molecules",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (a) PCl\u2085 (b) SF\u2086 (c) BrF\u2085 (d) BF\u2083
List-II: (i) Square pyramidal (ii) Trigonal planar (iii) Octahedral (iv) Trigonal bipyramidal`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],c:1,s:`PCl\u2085: 5 bond pairs, sp\xB3d, trigonal bipyramidal.
SF\u2086: 6 bond pairs, sp\xB3d\xB2, octahedral.
BrF\u2085: 5 bond pairs and 1 lone pair, sp\xB3d\xB2, square pyramidal.
BF\u2083: 3 bond pairs, sp\xB2, trigonal planar.
Match: a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2021-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Isothermal irreversible expansion",q:"For irreversible expansion of an ideal gas under isothermal condition, the correct option is:",o:["\u0394U \u2260 0, \u0394Stotal = 0","\u0394U = 0, \u0394Stotal = 0","\u0394U \u2260 0, \u0394Stotal \u2260 0","\u0394U = 0, \u0394Stotal \u2260 0"],c:3,s:`For an ideal gas, U depends only on T. The process is isothermal, so \u0394T = 0 and \u0394U = 0.
An irreversible process is spontaneous, so the entropy of the universe increases: \u0394Stotal > 0, which is not zero.
So \u0394U = 0 and \u0394Stotal \u2260 0.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2021-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Magnetic moment of complexes",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (a) [Fe(CN)\u2086]\xB3\u207B (b) [Fe(H\u2082O)\u2086]\xB3\u207A (c) [Fe(CN)\u2086]\u2074\u207B (d) [Fe(H\u2082O)\u2086]\xB2\u207A
List-II: (i) 5.92 BM (ii) 0 BM (iii) 4.90 BM (iv) 1.73 BM`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],c:0,s:`\u03BC = \u221A(n(n + 2)) BM, n = unpaired electrons. CN\u207B is a strong field ligand (pairs electrons); H\u2082O is weak (does not pair).
(a) Fe\xB3\u207A is d\u2075, low spin: n = 1, \u03BC = 1.73 BM.
(b) Fe\xB3\u207A d\u2075, high spin: n = 5, \u03BC = 5.92 BM.
(c) Fe\xB2\u207A d\u2076, low spin: n = 0, \u03BC = 0.
(d) Fe\xB2\u207A d\u2076, high spin: n = 4, \u03BC = 4.90 BM.
Match: a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2021-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Reduction with NaBH4",q:"The product formed in the following chemical reaction is:",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:0,s:`The reactant has a ring ketone and a CH\u2082COOCH\u2083 ester side chain (and a methyl on the ring).
NaBH\u2084 is a mild reducing agent: it reduces the ketone C=O to a secondary alcohol, but not the ester.
So the ring becomes C\u2013OH and the ester stays. That is (1).`,fig:{w:450,h:315,alt:"Reaction of a 2-methyl-6-(methoxycarbonylmethyl)cyclohexanone with NaBH4 in ethanol, with four possible products (1) to (4)",d:[["p","M45 44 L67.5 57 L67.5 83 L45 96 L22.5 83 L22.5 57 Z"],["l",43.3,44,43.3,34],["l",46.7,44,46.7,34],["t",45,30,"O","m"],["l",67.51599999999999,83,80.51599999999999,91],["t",92.51599999999999,99,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",67.51599999999999,57,82.51599999999999,38],["t",97.51599999999999,42,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",111.51599999999999,38,119.51599999999999,38],["t",128.516,42,"C","m"],["l",127.01599999999999,30,127.01599999999999,21],["l",130.016,30,130.016,21],["t",128.516,17,"O","m"],["l",137.516,38,144.516,38],["t",167.516,42,"OCH\u2083","m"],["l",215,62,262,62,"a"],["t",239,52,"NaBH\u2084","ms"],["t",239,78,"C\u2082H\u2085OH","ms"],["t",278,66,"?","m"],["p","M60 144 L82.5 157 L82.5 183 L60 196 L37.5 183 L37.5 157 Z"],["l",60,144,60,135],["t",60,132,"OH","m"],["l",82.51599999999999,183,95.51599999999999,191],["t",107.51599999999999,199,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",82.51599999999999,157,97.51599999999999,138],["t",112.51599999999999,142,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",126.51599999999999,138,134.516,138],["t",143.516,142,"C","m"],["l",142.016,130,142.016,121],["l",145.016,130,145.016,121],["t",143.516,117,"O","m"],["l",152.516,138,159.516,138],["t",182.516,142,"OCH\u2083","m"],["t",16,174,"(1)","s"],["p","M280 144 L302.5 157 L302.5 183 L280 196 L257.5 183 L257.5 157 Z"],["l",280,144,280,135],["t",280,132,"OH","m"],["l",302.516,183,315.516,191],["t",327.516,199,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",302.516,157,317.516,138],["t",332.516,142,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",346.516,138,354.516,138],["t",363.516,142,"C","m"],["l",363.516,130,363.516,124],["t",363.516,120,"H","m"],["l",363.516,146,363.516,152],["t",363.516,164,"OH","m"],["l",372.516,138,379.516,138],["t",402.516,142,"OCH\u2083","m"],["t",236,174,"(2)","s"],["p","M60 236 L82.5 249 L82.5 275 L60 288 L37.5 275 L37.5 249 Z"],["l",58.3,236,58.3,226],["l",61.7,236,61.7,226],["t",60,222,"O","m"],["l",82.51599999999999,275,95.51599999999999,283],["t",107.51599999999999,291,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",82.51599999999999,249,97.51599999999999,230],["t",112.51599999999999,234,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",126.51599999999999,230,134.516,230],["t",144.516,234,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",158.516,230,165.516,230],["t",178.516,234,"OH","m"],["t",16,266,"(3)","s"],["p","M280 236 L302.5 249 L302.5 275 L280 288 L257.5 275 L257.5 249 Z"],["l",280,236,280,227],["t",280,224,"OH","m"],["l",302.516,275,315.516,283],["t",327.516,291,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",302.516,249,317.516,230],["t",332.516,234,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",346.516,230,354.516,230],["t",363.516,234,"C","m"],["l",363.516,222,363.516,216],["t",363.516,212,"H","m"],["l",363.516,238,363.516,244],["t",363.516,256,"OH","m"],["l",372.516,230,379.516,230],["t",392.516,234,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",236,266,"(4)","s"]]},sfig:{w:450,h:115,alt:"NaBH4 in ethanol reduces the ring ketone to a ring alcohol while the ester side chain stays unchanged",d:[["p","M45 36 L65.8 48 L65.8 72 L45 84 L24.2 72 L24.2 48 Z"],["l",43.3,36,43.3,26],["l",46.7,36,46.7,26],["t",45,22,"O","m"],["l",65.78399999999999,72,78.78399999999999,80],["t",90.78399999999999,88,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",65.78399999999999,48,80.78399999999999,30],["t",95.78399999999999,34,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",109.78399999999999,30,117.78399999999999,30],["t",126.78399999999999,34,"C","m"],["l",125.28399999999999,22,125.28399999999999,13],["l",128.284,22,128.284,13],["t",126.78399999999999,9,"O","m"],["l",135.784,30,142.784,30],["t",165.784,34,"OCH\u2083","m"],["l",205,56,258,56,"a"],["t",231,46,"NaBH\u2084","ms"],["t",231,72,"C\u2082H\u2085OH","ms"],["p","M300 36 L320.8 48 L320.8 72 L300 84 L279.2 72 L279.2 48 Z"],["l",300,36,300,27],["t",300,24,"OH","m"],["l",320.784,72,333.784,80],["t",345.784,88,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",320.784,48,335.784,30],["t",350.784,34,"CH\u2082","m"],["l",364.784,30,372.784,30],["t",381.784,34,"C","m"],["l",380.284,22,380.284,13],["l",383.284,22,383.284,13],["t",381.784,9,"O","m"],["l",390.784,30,397.784,30],["t",420.784,34,"OCH\u2083","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2021-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Trends in group properties",q:"In which one of the following arrangements the given sequence is not strictly according to the properties indicated against it?",o:["CO\u2082 < SiO\u2082 < SnO\u2082 < PbO\u2082 : Increasing oxiding power","HF < HCl < HBr < HI : Increasing acidic strength","H\u2082O < H\u2082S < H\u2082Se < H\u2082Te : Increasing pKa values","NH\u2083 < PH\u2083 < AsH\u2083 < SbH\u2083 : Increasing acidic character"],c:2,s:`Going down group 16, the E\u2013H bond weakens, so the hydrides become stronger acids.
A stronger acid has a lower pKa, so the pKa order is H\u2082O > H\u2082S > H\u2082Se > H\u2082Te (decreasing).
So sequence (3), which says increasing pKa, is wrong. The other three are correct.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2021-q090",pn:90,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Decarboxylation",q:`CH\u2083CH\u2082COO\u207BNa\u207A \u2192(NaOH, +, Heat) CH\u2083CH\u2083 + Na\u2082CO\u2083.
Consider the above reaction and identify the missing reagent/chemical.`,o:["DIBAL-H","B\u2082H\u2086","Red Phosphorus","CaO"],c:3,s:`Heating a sodium salt of a carboxylic acid with soda lime (NaOH + CaO, ratio 3:1) removes CO\u2082 as Na\u2082CO\u2083 and gives an alkane with one carbon less.
CH\u2083CH\u2082COONa + NaOH \u2192 CH\u2083CH\u2083 + Na\u2082CO\u2083.
The missing chemical is CaO.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2021-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Polarity of molecules",q:"Which of the following molecules is non-polar in nature?",o:["NO\u2082","POCl\u2083","CH\u2082O","SbCl\u2085"],c:3,s:`SbCl\u2085 is trigonal bipyramidal and symmetric, so the bond dipoles cancel: non-polar.
NO\u2082 is bent, POCl\u2083 is tetrahedral with one P=O and three P\u2013Cl bonds, and CH\u2082O has a C=O dipole not balanced by C\u2013H. These are polar.`,sfig:{w:320,h:405,alt:"SbCl5 as a trigonal bipyramid with equal bond dipole arrows that cancel, and NO2, POCl3 and CH2O with an unbalanced dipole, so polar",d:[["t",6,68,"SbCl\u2085 :","s"],["t",130,68,"Sb","m"],["t",130,18,"Cl","m"],["l",130,53,130,25],["l",135,51,135,26,"a"],["t",130,118,"Cl","m"],["l",130,75,130,103],["l",125,77,125,102,"a"],["t",74,68,"Cl","m"],["l",119,64,85,64],["l",117,59,86,59,"a"],["t",192,38,"Cl","m"],["l",139.90175176557065,59.20882979085291,182.09824823442935,38.79117020914709],["l",143.87987490892309,62.83850419172192,183.37589362353523,43.72752739432894,"a"],["t",192,98,"Cl","m"],["l",139.90175176557065,68.79117020914708,182.09824823442935,89.20882979085292],["l",139.52426562788028,74.16308832243321,179.02028434249243,93.2740651198262,"a"],["t",6,180,"NO\u2082 :","s"],["t",100,180,"N","m"],["t",85,180,"\u2299","s"],["t",140,158,"O","m"],["t",140,202,"O","m"],["l",107.01848942849993,170.08554261643263,131.24660307232028,156.76008011233142],["l",108.7533969276797,173.23991988766858,132.98151057150005,159.91445738356737],["l",107.88594317808982,180.3372687479494,132.11405682191017,193.6627312520506,"a"],["t",190,180,"\u21D2 polar molecule","s"],["t",6,266,"POCl\u2083 :","s"],["t",130,266,"P","m"],["t",130,230,"O","m"],["l",128.2,253,128.2,235],["l",131.8,253,131.8,235],["t",98,288,"Cl","m"],["l",122.58362268220569,267.09875940598357,106.24041924199368,278.3347117711293],["t",162,288,"Cl","m"],["l",137.4163773177943,267.09875940598357,153.75958075800634,278.3347117711293],["t",130,304,"Cl","m"],["l",130,271,130,290],["t",190,274,"\u21D2 polar molecule","s"],["t",6,364,"CH\u2082O :","s"],["t",130,364,"C","m"],["t",130,328,"O","m"],["l",128.2,351,128.2,333],["l",131.8,351,131.8,333],["t",104,386,"H","m"],["l",123.12952343167797,365.81348017319556,110.87047656832203,376.18651982680444],["t",156,386,"H","m"],["l",136.87047656832203,365.81348017319556,149.12952343167797,376.18651982680444],["t",190,376,"\u21D2 polar molecule","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2021-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Atmospheric pollution reactions",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (a) 2SO\u2082(g) + O\u2082(g) \u2192 2SO\u2083(g) (b) HOCl(g) \u2192(h\u03BD) \u2022OH + \u2022Cl (c) CaCO\u2083 + H\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 CaSO\u2084 + H\u2082O + CO\u2082 (d) NO\u2082(g) \u2192(h\u03BD) NO(g) + O(g)
List-II: (i) Acid rain (ii) Smog (iii) Ozone depletion (iv) Tropospheric pollution`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],c:3,s:`(a) Oxidation of SO\u2082 to SO\u2083 by pollutants in the lower air: tropospheric pollution (iv).
(b) Sunlight splits HOCl into chlorine radicals that destroy ozone: ozone depletion (iii).
(c) Sulphuric acid from acid rain attacks marble (CaCO\u2083): acid rain (i).
(d) Sunlight splits NO\u2082 into NO and O, which leads to photochemical smog (ii).
Match: a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2021-q093",pn:93,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Dissociation constant from conductivity",q:`The molar conductivity of 0.007 M acetic acid is 20 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9. What is the dissociation constant of acetic acid? Choose the correct option.
[\u039B\xB0(H\u207A) = 350 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9, \u039B\xB0(CH\u2083COO\u207B) = 50 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9]`,o:["2.50 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 mol L\u207B\xB9","1.75 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 mol L\u207B\xB9","2.50 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 mol L\u207B\xB9","1.75 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 mol L\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`\u039B\xB0(CH\u2083COOH) = 350 + 50 = 400 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.
Degree of dissociation \u03B1 = \u039Bm/\u039B\xB0 = 20/400 = 1/20 = 0.05.
\u03B1 is small, so Ka \u2248 C\u03B1\xB2.
Ka = 0.007 \xD7 (0.05)\xB2 = 7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 1.75 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 mol L\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2021-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Dalton's law of partial pressures",q:"Choose the correct option for the total pressure (in atm.) in a mixture of 4 g O\u2082 and 2 g H\u2082 confined in a total volume of one litre at 0\xB0C is : [Given R = 0.082 L atm mol\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB9, T = 273 K]",o:["26.02","2.518","2.602","25.18"],c:3,s:`Moles O\u2082 = 4/32 = 1/8 = 0.125.
Moles H\u2082 = 2/2 = 1.
Total moles = 9/8 = 1.125.
PV = nRT, so P = 1.125 \xD7 0.082 \xD7 273 / 1 = 25.18 atm.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"States of Matter"},{id:"neet-2021-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Solutions",st:"Raoult's law for liquid mixtures",q:"The correct option for the value of vapour pressure of a solution at 45\xB0C with benzene to octane in molar ratio 3 : 2 is : [At 45\xB0C vapour pressure of benzene is 280 mm Hg and that of octane is 420 mm Hg. Assume Ideal gas]",o:["350 mm of Hg","160 mm of Hg","168 mm of Hg","336 mm of Hg"],c:3,s:`Mole fractions: benzene 3/5, octane 2/5.
Total vapour pressure = p\xB0(benzene) x(benzene) + p\xB0(octane) x(octane).
= 280 \xD7 3/5 + 420 \xD7 2/5 = 168 + 168.
= 336 mm Hg.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2021-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Isoelectronic species",q:"From the following pairs of ions which one is not an iso-electronic pair?",o:["Fe\xB2\u207A, Mn\xB2\u207A","O\xB2\u207B, F\u207B","Na\u207A, Mg\xB2\u207A","Mn\xB2\u207A, Fe\xB3\u207A"],c:0,s:`Isoelectronic ions have the same number of electrons.
Fe\xB2\u207A = 26 \u2013 2 = 24; Mn\xB2\u207A = 25 \u2013 2 = 23. Different.
O\xB2\u207B = 10, F\u207B = 10; Na\u207A = 10, Mg\xB2\u207A = 10; Mn\xB2\u207A = 23, Fe\xB3\u207A = 23.
So Fe\xB2\u207A, Mn\xB2\u207A is not an isoelectronic pair.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2021-q097",pn:97,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Etard reaction",q:"The intermediate compound 'X' in the following chemical reaction is:",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:1,s:`Toluene with chromyl chloride (CrO\u2082Cl\u2082) in CS\u2082 is the Etard reaction.
The methyl group forms a chromium complex, C\u2086H\u2085CH(OCrOHCl\u2082)\u2082 (X).
Hydrolysis with H\u2083O\u207A then gives benzaldehyde.
So X is option (2).`,fig:{w:470,h:215,alt:"Etard reaction: toluene with CrO2Cl2 in CS2 gives intermediate X, which with H3O+ gives benzaldehyde; four options for X are shown as benzene rings carrying CHClH, CH(OCrOHCl2)2, CH(OCOCH3)2 or CHCl2",d:[["p","M28 37 L41 44.5 L41 59.5 L28 67 L15 59.5 L15 44.5 Z"],["c",28,52,8.25],["l",40.99,44.5,49.99,35.5],["t",60.99,32.5,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",52,70,"+ CrO\u2082Cl\u2082","s"],["l",122,56,172,56,"a"],["t",147,48,"CS\u2082","ms"],["t",186,60,"X","m"],["l",200,56,250,56,"a"],["t",225,48,"H\u2083O\u207A","ms"],["p","M282 41 L295 48.5 L295 63.5 L282 71 L269 63.5 L269 48.5 Z"],["c",282,56,8.25],["l",294.99,48.5,302.99,42.5],["t",308.99,43.5,"C","m"],["l",307.19,31.5,307.19,19.5],["l",310.79,31.5,310.79,19.5],["t",308.99,16.5,"O","m"],["l",316.99,40.5,323.99,40.5],["t",330.99,43.5,"H","m"],["t",18,131,"(1)","s"],["p","M50 110 L63 117.5 L63 132.5 L50 140 L37 132.5 L37 117.5 Z"],["c",50,125,8.25],["l",62.99,117.5,70.99000000000001,113.5],["t",78.99000000000001,115.5,"CH","m"],["l",86.99000000000001,107.5,96.99000000000001,97.5],["t",103.99000000000001,98.5,"Cl","m"],["l",86.99000000000001,114.5,94.99000000000001,124.5],["t",99.99000000000001,134.5,"H","m"],["t",248,131,"(2)","s"],["p","M280 110 L293 117.5 L293 132.5 L280 140 L267 132.5 L267 117.5 Z"],["c",280,125,8.25],["l",292.99,117.5,300.99,113.5],["t",346.99,115.5,"CH(OCrOHCl\u2082)\u2082","m"],["t",18,196,"(3)","s"],["p","M50 175 L63 182.5 L63 197.5 L50 205 L37 197.5 L37 182.5 Z"],["c",50,190,8.25],["l",62.99,182.5,70.99000000000001,178.5],["t",114.99000000000001,180.5,"CH(OCOCH\u2083)\u2082","m"],["t",248,196,"(4)","s"],["p","M280 175 L293 182.5 L293 197.5 L280 205 L267 197.5 L267 182.5 Z"],["c",280,190,8.25],["l",292.99,182.5,300.99,178.5],["t",308.99,180.5,"CH","m"],["l",316.99,172.5,326.99,162.5],["t",333.99,163.5,"Cl","m"],["l",316.99,179.5,326.99,189.5],["t",333.99,198.5,"Cl","m"]]},sfig:{w:500,h:120,alt:"Etard reaction: toluene with CrO2Cl2 in CS2 gives the intermediate X, C6H5CH(OCrOHCl2)2, and H3O+ then gives benzaldehyde",d:[["p","M26 47 L39 54.5 L39 69.5 L26 77 L13 69.5 L13 54.5 Z"],["c",26,62,8.25],["l",38.99,54.5,47.99,45.5],["t",58.99,42.5,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",56,80,"+ CrO\u2082Cl\u2082","s"],["l",125,66,172,66,"a"],["t",148,58,"CS\u2082","ms"],["p","M215 51 L228 58.5 L228 73.5 L215 81 L202 73.5 L202 58.5 Z"],["c",215,66,8.25],["l",227.99,58.5,237.99,48.5],["t",243.99,50.5,"CH","m"],["l",252.99,41.5,261.99,31.5],["t",264.99,35.5,"(OCrOHCl\u2082)","s"],["l",252.99,50.5,261.99,60.5],["t",264.99,68.5,"(OCrOHCl\u2082)","s"],["t",215,108,"(X)","m"],["l",345,66,392,66,"a"],["t",368,58,"H\u2083O\u207A","ms"],["p","M425 51 L438 58.5 L438 73.5 L425 81 L412 73.5 L412 58.5 Z"],["c",425,66,8.25],["l",437.99,58.5,445.99,52.5],["t",451.99,53.5,"C","m"],["l",450.19,41.5,450.19,29.5],["l",453.79,41.5,453.79,29.5],["t",451.99,26.5,"O","m"],["l",459.99,50.5,466.99,50.5],["t",473.99,53.5,"H","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2021-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Amines",st:"Diazonium salt reactions",q:"The reagent 'R' in the given sequence of chemical reaction is:",o:["CuCN/KCN","H\u2082O","CH\u2083CH\u2082OH","HI"],c:2,s:`The starting material is 2,4,6-tribromoaniline. NaNO\u2082/HCl at 0\u20135 \xB0C changes it to the diazonium chloride.
The final product has the same ring but with H in place of the N\u2082\u207A group: 1,3,5-tribromobenzene.
Replacing N\u2082\u207A by H (reduction) is done by ethanol (or H\u2083PO\u2082).
CuCN/KCN would add CN, H\u2082O would give a phenol, HI would give an iodide. So R is CH\u2083CH\u2082OH.`,fig:{w:440,h:125,alt:"2,4,6-tribromoaniline reacts with NaNO2 and HCl at 0-5 degrees C to give the diazonium chloride, which reacts with reagent R to give 1,3,5-tribromobenzene",d:[["p","M55 46 L72.3 56 L72.3 76 L55 86 L37.7 76 L37.7 56 Z"],["l",56.704288,50.584,67.49811199999999,56.815999999999995],["l",67.49811199999999,75.184,56.704288,81.41600000000001],["l",40.7976,72.232,40.7976,59.768],["l",55,46,55,35],["t",55,32,"NH\u2082","m"],["l",37.68,56,28.88,52.7],["t",21.68,54,"Br","m"],["l",72.32,56,81.11999999999999,52.7],["t",88.32,54,"Br","m"],["l",55,86,55,97],["t",55,108,"Br","m"],["l",105,66,170,66,"a"],["t",137,56,"NaNO\u2082, HCl","ms"],["t",137,88,"0-5\xB0C","ms"],["p","M225 46 L242.3 56 L242.3 76 L225 86 L207.7 76 L207.7 56 Z"],["l",226.704288,50.584,237.498112,56.815999999999995],["l",237.498112,75.184,226.704288,81.41600000000001],["l",210.79760000000002,72.232,210.79760000000002,59.768],["l",225,46,225,35],["t",225,32,"N\u2082\u207A Cl\u207B","m"],["l",207.68,56,198.88,52.7],["t",191.68,54,"Br","m"],["l",242.32,56,251.12,52.7],["t",258.32,54,"Br","m"],["l",225,86,225,97],["t",225,108,"Br","m"],["l",275,66,330,66,"a"],["t",302,58,"R","ms"],["p","M385 46 L402.3 56 L402.3 76 L385 86 L367.7 76 L367.7 56 Z"],["l",386.704288,50.584,397.498112,56.815999999999995],["l",397.498112,75.184,386.704288,81.41600000000001],["l",370.7976,72.232,370.7976,59.768],["l",367.68,56,358.88,52.7],["t",351.68,54,"Br","m"],["l",402.32,56,411.12,52.7],["t",418.32,54,"Br","m"],["l",385,86,385,97],["t",385,108,"Br","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2021-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius plot",q:"The slope of Arrhenius plot (ln k vs 1/T) of first order reaction is \u20135 \xD7 10\xB3K. The value of Ea of the reaction is. Choose the correct option for your answer. [Given R = 8.314 JK\u207B\xB9mol\u207B\xB9]",o:["\u201383 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","41.5 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","83.0 kJ mol\u207B\xB9","166 kJ mol\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`Arrhenius: ln k = ln A \u2013 (Ea/R)(1/T).
So the slope of ln k against 1/T is \u2013Ea/R.
\u20135 \xD7 10\xB3 = \u2013Ea/8.314.
Ea = 5 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 8.314 = 41570 J mol\u207B\xB9 \u2248 41.5 kJ mol\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2021-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Named reactions",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) benzene + CO, HCl with anhydrous AlCl\u2083/CuCl (b) R\u2013CO\u2013CH\u2083 + NaOX (c) R\u2013CH\u2082\u2013OH + R'COOH with conc. H\u2082SO\u2084 (d) R\u2013CH\u2082COOH with (i) X\u2082/red P, (ii) H\u2082O
List-II: (i) Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction (ii) Gattermann-Koch reaction (iii) Haloform reaction (iv) Esterification`,o:["(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (i), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (ii)"],c:0,s:`(a) Benzene with CO and HCl over AlCl\u2083/CuCl gives benzaldehyde: Gattermann-Koch.
(b) A methyl ketone with NaOX gives a carboxylate and CHX\u2083: haloform reaction.
(c) Alcohol + carboxylic acid with conc. H\u2082SO\u2084 gives an ester: esterification.
(d) A carboxylic acid with X\u2082/red P gives the \u03B1-halo acid: Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky.
Match: a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2021-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Ecological pyramids",q:"Which of the following statements is not correct ?",o:["Pyramid of numbers in a grassland ecosystem is upright.","Pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted.","Pyramid of biomass in sea is generally upright.","Pyramid of energy is always upright."],c:2,s:`In the sea the producers are tiny phytoplankton that multiply fast but have a short life, so their standing biomass is small.
Zooplankton and other consumers live longer, so their biomass is larger.
So the sea biomass pyramid is usually inverted, and statement 3 (upright) is wrong.
A grassland number pyramid is upright, and an energy pyramid is always upright, so those statements are correct.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2021-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Sexuality in plants",q:"Which of the following plants is monoecious?",o:["Cycas circinalis","Carica papaya","Chara","Marchantia polymorpha"],c:2,s:`A monoecious plant has male and female sex organs on the same plant.
Most Chara species are monoecious.
Cycas, papaya and Marchantia have male and female organs on different plants (dioecious).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"C4 pathway",q:"The first stable product of CO\u2082 fixation in Sorghum is",o:["Phosphoglyceric acid","Pyruvic acid","Oxaloacetic acid","Succinic acid"],c:2,s:`Sorghum is a C4 plant.
In C4 plants, CO\u2082 is first fixed by PEP carboxylase in mesophyll cells, giving the 4-carbon oxaloacetic acid.
PGA is the first product in C3 plants; pyruvic acid ends glycolysis; succinic acid is a Krebs cycle intermediate.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Androecium",q:"Diadelphous stamens are found in",o:["China rose and citrus","China rose","Citrus","Pea"],c:3,s:`Diadelphous stamens are joined into two bundles.
Pea has this (9 + 1 stamens).
China rose has one bundle (monoadelphous) and citrus has many bundles (polyadelphous).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"DNA strands on a gel stained with ethidium bromide when viewed under UV radiation, appear as",o:["Bright blue bands","Yellow bands","Bright orange bands","Dark red bands"],c:2,s:`Ethidium bromide slips between the DNA bases and fluoresces.
Under UV light the stained DNA bands glow bright orange.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2021-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Plasticity",q:"Plants follow different pathways in response to environment or phases of life to form different kinds of structures. This ability is called",o:["Maturity","Elasticity","Flexibility","Plasticity"],c:3,s:"The ability of a plant to form different structures in response to environment or life phase is called plasticity (for example heterophylly).",sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2021-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Plant tissue culture",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Protoplast fusion (b) Plant tissue culture (c) Meristem culture (d) Micropropagation
List-II: (i) Totipotency (ii) Pomato (iii) Somaclones (iv) Virus free plants`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],c:2,s:`Protoplast fusion of potato and tomato gives pomato: a-ii.
Plant tissue culture works because cells are totipotent: b-i.
Meristem tips are virus free, so meristem culture gives virus-free plants: c-iv.
Micropropagation produces genetically identical plants, somaclones: d-iii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2021-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Primary productivity",q:"In the equation GPP \u2013 R = NPP ; R represents :",o:["Respiration losses","Radiant energy","Retardation factor","Environmental factor"],c:0,s:`GPP is gross primary productivity, the total food made.
Plants use some of it in respiration (R).
What remains is NPP, so R stands for respiration losses.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2021-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Punnett square",q:"The production of gametes by the parents, formation of zygotes, the F\u2081 and F\u2082 plants, can be understood from a diagram called :",o:["Net square","Bullet square","Punch square","Punnett square"],c:3,s:"The chart that shows gametes of the parents, zygote formation and the F\u2081 and F\u2082 offspring is the Punnett square, devised by Reginald Punnett.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2021-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Cell structures",q:"Which of the following is an incorrect statement?",o:["Nuclear pores act as passages for proteins and RNA molecules in both directions between nucleus and cytoplasm","Mature sieve tube elements possess a conspicuous nucleus and usual cytoplasmic organelles","Microbodies are present both in plant and animal cells","The perinuclear space forms a barrier between the materials present inside the nucleus and that of the cytoplasm"],c:1,s:`Mature sieve tube elements have only a thin peripheral cytoplasm and a large vacuole; they have no nucleus.
So statement 2 is incorrect.
The other three statements are correct.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2021-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Secondary growth",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Lenticels (b) Cork cambium (c) Secondary cortex (d) Cork
List-II: (i) Phellogen (ii) Suberin deposition (iii) Exchange of gases (iv) Phelloderm`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:2,s:`Lenticels allow gas exchange: a-iii.
Cork cambium is also called phellogen: b-i.
Secondary cortex is the phelloderm: c-iv.
Cork cells are filled with suberin: d-ii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Standing state",q:"The amount of nutrients, such as carbon, nitrogen, phosphorus and calcium present in the soil at any given time, is referred as :",o:["Standing crop","Climax","Climax community","Standing state"],c:3,s:`The amount of inorganic nutrients in the soil at a given time is the standing state.
Standing crop is the living material at each trophic level, and climax community is the final stable stage of succession.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2021-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Types of pollination",q:"The term used for transfer of pollen grains from anthers of one plant to stigma of a different plant which, during pollination, brings genetically different types of pollen grains to stigma, is :",o:["Cleistogamy","Xenogamy","Geitonogamy","Chasmogamy"],c:1,s:`Pollen moving from one plant to the stigma of a different plant is xenogamy; it brings genetically different pollen.
Geitonogamy is between flowers of the same plant.
Cleistogamy and chasmogamy are about closed and open flowers.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis",q:"Which of the following stages of meiosis involves division of centromere?",o:["Telophase II","Metaphase I","Metaphase II","Anaphase II"],c:3,s:`In anaphase II the centromeres split, so sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles.
In metaphase I and II chromosomes only line up; telophase II is when they reach the poles and uncoil.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2021-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR",q:"Which of the following is a correct sequence of steps in a PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)?",o:["Annealing, Denaturation, Extension","Denaturation, Annealing, Extension","Denaturation, Extension, Annealing","Extension, Denaturation, Annealing"],c:1,s:`Step 1, denaturation: heating (about 94 \xB0C) separates the DNA strands.
Step 2, annealing: primers bind to the single strands.
Step 3, extension: Taq polymerase adds nucleotides to the primers.
Order: denaturation, annealing, extension.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2021-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Gene therapy",q:"When gene targetting involving gene amplification is attempted in an individual's tissue to treat disease, it is known as :",o:["Safety testing","Biopiracy","Gene therapy","Molecular diagnosis"],c:2,s:`Gene therapy corrects a gene defect by delivering a working gene into the individual's cells.
Biopiracy is unauthorised use of bio-resources, and molecular diagnosis is only identifying a disease.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2021-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Cell organelles",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (a) Cristae (b) Thylakoids (c) Centromere (d) Cisternae
List-II: (i) Primary constriction in chromosome (ii) Disc-shaped sacs in Golgi apparatus (iii) Infoldings in mitochondria (iv) Flattened membranous sacs in stroma of plastids`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],c:3,s:`Cristae are infoldings of the inner mitochondrial membrane: a-iii.
Thylakoids are flattened sacs in the stroma of plastids: b-iv.
The centromere is the primary constriction of a chromosome: c-i.
Cisternae are the disc-shaped sacs of the Golgi: d-ii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2021-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Evolution",st:"Founder effect",q:"The factor that leads to Founder effect in a population is :",o:["Genetic drift","Natural selection","Genetic recombination","Mutation"],c:0,s:`When a few individuals start a new isolated population, chance changes in allele frequency follow. This chance change is genetic drift.
Founder effect is one form of genetic drift (the other is the bottleneck effect).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2021-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Chromosome types",q:"When the centromere is situated in the middle of two equal arms of chromosomes, the chromosome is referred as :",o:["Acrocentric","Metacentric","Telocentric","Sub-metacentric"],c:1,s:`Centromere in the middle gives two equal arms: metacentric.
Slightly off-centre: sub-metacentric. Near one end: acrocentric. At the end: telocentric.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2021-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Asexual reproduction in bryophytes",q:"Gemmae are present in",o:["Some Liverworts","Mosses","Pteridophytes","Some Gymnosperms"],c:0,s:`Gemmae are green multicellular buds made in gemma cups of some liverworts such as Marchantia, used for asexual reproduction.
Mosses multiply by fragmentation and budding of protonema.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Transport in Plants",st:"Properties of water",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Cohesion (b) Adhesion (c) Surface tension (d) Guttation
List-II: (i) More attraction in liquid phase (ii) Mutual attraction among water molecules (iii) Water loss in liquid phase (iv) Attraction towards polar surfaces`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],c:1,s:`Cohesion is the mutual attraction among water molecules: a-ii.
Adhesion is attraction towards polar surfaces such as the xylem wall: b-iv.
Surface tension: molecules feel more attraction in the liquid than in the gas phase: c-i.
Guttation is loss of water as liquid from leaf margins: d-iii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Transport in Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Photoperiodism",q:"The site of perception of light in plants during photoperiodism is",o:["Leaf","Shoot apex","Stem","Axillary bud"],c:0,s:`The photoperiodic stimulus is perceived by the leaves.
The shoot apex is where the low-temperature stimulus is perceived in vernalisation.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2021-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Auxins as weedicides",q:"The plant hormone used to destroy weeds in a field",o:["IBA","IAA","NAA","2, 4-D"],c:3,s:`2,4-D is a synthetic auxin used as a weedicide; it kills broad-leaved (dicot) weeds in cereal fields.
IAA and IBA are natural auxins, and NAA is a synthetic auxin used for rooting and fruit setting.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2021-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Algae",q:"Which of the following algae produce Carrageen?",o:["Blue-green algae","Green algae","Brown algae","Red algae"],c:3,s:`The red algae (Rhodophyceae) give agar and carrageen from their cell walls.
Brown algae give algin.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Central dogma",q:`Complete the flow chart on central dogma.
(a) DNA (circular arrow on DNA) \u2192(b) mRNA \u2192(c) (d)`,o:["(a)-Transduction; (b)-Translation; (c)-Replication ; (d)-Protein","(a)-Replication; (b)-Transcription ; (c)-Transduction; (d)-Protein","(a)-Translation; (b)-Replication; (c)-Transcription;(d)-Transduction","(a)-Replication; (b)-Transcription; (c)-Translation; (d)-Protein"],c:3,s:`The circular arrow on DNA is DNA making DNA: replication (a).
DNA to mRNA is transcription (b).
mRNA to protein is translation (c), and the product (d) is protein.
Transduction is gene transfer by a virus between bacteria, so it is not in the dogma.`,fig:{w:320,h:72,alt:"Central dogma flow chart: (a) DNA with a circular arrow on it, arrow (b) to mRNA, arrow (c) to a box labelled (d)",d:[["t",14,44,"(a)","m"],["p","M70.3,23 A30 16 0 1 1 49.7,23","a"],["t",60,44,"DNA","m"],["l",98,38,150,38,"a"],["t",121,26,"(b)","ms"],["t",176,42,"mRNA","m"],["l",208,38,260,38,"a"],["t",234,26,"(c)","ms"],["t",284,42,"(d)","m"]]},sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Secondary metabolites",q:"Which of the following are not secondary metabolites in plants?",o:["Rubber, gums","Morphine, codeine","Amino acids, glucose","Vinblastin, curcumin"],c:2,s:`Amino acids and glucose have known roles in normal metabolism, so they are primary metabolites.
Rubber, gums, morphine, codeine, vinblastine and curcumin are secondary metabolites.
So option 3 is not secondary.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2021-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Interspecific competition",q:"Inspite of interspecific competition in nature, which mechanism the competing species might have evolved for their survival ?",o:["Predation","Resource partitioning","Competitive release","Mutualism"],c:1,s:`Competing species can survive together by using the resource differently, for example feeding at different times or on different parts. This is resource partitioning.
Competitive release is a rise in one species when its competitor is removed.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2021-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Applications of PCR",q:"Which of the following is not an application of PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction) ?",o:["Detection of gene mutation","Molecular diagnosis","Gene amplification","Purification of isolated protein"],c:3,s:`PCR makes many copies of a DNA segment.
It is used to amplify genes, detect mutations and detect infectious agents (molecular diagnosis).
It works on DNA, not protein, so it is not used to purify proteins.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2021-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Tissues",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Cells with active cell division capacity (b) Tissue having all cells similar in structure and function (c) Tissue having different types of cells (d) Dead cells with highly thickened walls and narrow lumen
List-II: (i) Vascular tissues (ii) Meristematic tissue (iii) Sclereids (iv) Simple tissue`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],c:1,s:`Cells that divide actively form meristematic tissue: a-ii.
A tissue of similar cells is simple tissue: b-iv.
A tissue of different cell types is a complex tissue such as vascular tissue: c-i.
Dead cells with thick walls and narrow lumen are sclereids: d-iii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Embryo sac",q:"A typical angiosperm embryo sac at maturity is:",o:["8-nucleate and 8-celled","8-nucleate and 7-celled","7-nucleate and 8-celled","7-nucleate and 7-celled"],c:1,s:`The mature embryo sac has 7 cells: 3 antipodals, 2 synergids, 1 egg and 1 large central cell.
The central cell has 2 polar nuclei, so the total number of nuclei is 3 + 2 + 1 + 2 = 8.
So it is 8-nucleate and 7-celled.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Algae reserve food",q:"Which of the following algae contains mannitol as reserve food material?",o:["Ulothrix","Ectocarpus","Gracilaria","Volvox"],c:1,s:`Ectocarpus is a brown alga; brown algae store mannitol and laminarin.
Ulothrix and Volvox (green) store starch; Gracilaria (red) stores floridean starch.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Heterospory",q:"Genera like Selaginella and Salvinia produce two kinds of spores. Such plants are known as:",o:["Heterosporous","Homosorus","Heterosorus","Homosporous"],c:0,s:`Selaginella and Salvinia make small microspores and large megaspores, two kinds of spores.
Plants like this are heterosporous; homosporous plants make one kind.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mutagens",q:"Mutations in plant cells can be induced by:",o:["Zeatin","Kinetin","Infrared rays","Gamma rays"],c:3,s:`Gamma rays are ionising radiation and are physical mutagens that cause mutations.
Zeatin and kinetin are cytokinins (hormones), and infrared rays mainly heat.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2021-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:"Amensalism can be represented as:",o:["Species A (+); Species B (0)","Species A (\u2013); Species B (0)","Species A (+); Species B (+)","Species A (\u2013); Species B (\u2013)"],c:1,s:`In amensalism one species is harmed while the other is unaffected: (\u2013, 0).
(+, 0) is commensalism, (+, +) is mutualism, (\u2013, \u2013) is competition.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2021-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Secondary growth tissues",q:`Select the correct pair.
1. Loose parenchyma cells rupturing the epidermis and forming a lens shaped opening in bark \u2014 Spongy parenchyma
2. Large colorless empty cells in the epidermis of grass leaves \u2014 Subsidiary cells
3. In dicot leaves, vascular bundles are surrounded by large thick-walled cells \u2014 Conjunctive tissue
4. Cells of medullary rays that form part of cambial ring \u2014 Interfascicular cambium`,o:["1","2","3","4"],c:3,s:`Pair 1 describes complementary cells of a lenticel, not spongy parenchyma.
Pair 2 describes bulliform cells, not subsidiary cells.
Pair 3 describes bundle sheath cells, not conjunctive tissue.
Pair 4 is correct: medullary ray cells between bundles become interfascicular cambium and join the cambial ring.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Insertional inactivation",q:"Plasmid pBR322 has PstI restriction enzyme site within gene ampR that confers ampicillin resistance. If this enzyme is used for inserting a gene for \u03B2-galactoside production and the recombinant plasmid is inserted in an E.coli strain",o:["It will be able to produce a novel protein with dual ability","It will not be able to confer ampicillin resistance to the host cell","The transformed cells will have the ability to resist ampicillin as well as produce \u03B2-galactoside","It will lead to lysis of host cell"],c:1,s:`PstI cuts inside the ampR gene.
The new gene is inserted into that gene, so ampR is broken (insertional inactivation).
The host therefore loses ampicillin resistance; it makes \u03B2-galactosidase, but this is neither a novel dual protein nor does it cause lysis.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2021-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"DNA fingerprinting",q:"DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence, called as",o:["Polymorphic DNA","Satellite DNA","Repetitive DNA","Single nucleotides"],c:2,s:`DNA fingerprinting compares short repeated sequences, called repetitive DNA, which differ between individuals.
(The printed key is repetitive DNA.)`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population growth",q:"In the exponential growth equation Nt = N\u2080 e\u02B3\u1D57, e represents",o:["The base of geometric logarithms","The base of number logarithms","The base of exponential logarithms","The base of natural logarithms"],c:3,s:`In Nt = N\u2080 e^(rt), N\u2080 is the starting density, r the intrinsic rate of increase and t the time.
The constant e (about 2.718) is the base of natural logarithms.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2021-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Light reaction",q:"Which of the following statements is incorrect ?",o:["Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I and PS II","Both ATP and NADPH + H\u207A are synthesized during non-cyclic photophosphorylation","Stroma lamellae have PS I only and lack NADP reductase","Grana lamellae have both PS I and PS II"],c:0,s:`Cyclic photophosphorylation uses only PS I, so statement 1 is wrong.
Non-cyclic flow uses both photosystems and makes ATP and NADPH.
Grana lamellae have PS I and PS II; stroma lamellae have only PS I and lack NADP reductase.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Terms in classification",q:"Which of the following statements is correct ?",o:["Some of the organisms can fix atmospheric nitrogen in specialized cells called sheath cells","Fusion of two cells is called Karyogamy","Fusion of protoplasms between two motile on non-motile gametes is called plasmogamy","Organisms that depend on living plants are called saprophytes"],c:2,s:`Nitrogen is fixed in specialised cells called heterocysts, not sheath cells, so 1 is wrong.
Karyogamy is fusion of two nuclei, not cells, so 2 is wrong.
Plasmogamy is fusion of protoplasm of two gametes, so 3 is right.
Organisms living on living plants are parasites; saprophytes use dead matter, so 4 is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2021-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Mineral Nutrition",st:"Nitrogen cycle",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Nitrococcus (b) Rhizobium (c) Thiobacillus (d) Nitrobacter
List-II: (i) Denitrification (ii) Conversion of ammonia to nitrite (iii) Conversion of nitrite to nitrate (iv) Conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],c:1,s:`Nitrococcus turns NH\u2083 into nitrite: a-ii.
Rhizobium fixes N\u2082 into ammonia: b-iv.
Thiobacillus denitrifies nitrate back to N\u2082: c-i.
Nitrobacter turns nitrite into nitrate: d-iii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Mineral Nutrition"},{id:"neet-2021-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Electron transport chain",q:"Which of the following statements of incorrect?",o:["Oxidation-reducation reactions produce proton gradient in respiration","During aerobic respiration, role of oxygen is limited to the terminal stage","In ETC (Electron Transport Chain), one molecule of NADH + H\u207A gives rise to 2 ATP molecules, and one FADH\u2082 gives rise to 3 ATP molecules","ATP is synthesized through complex V"],c:2,s:`The NCERT values are 3 ATP per NADH and 2 ATP per FADH\u2082, so statement 3 has them swapped and is incorrect.
Redox reactions build the proton gradient, O\u2082 is the final electron acceptor, and ATP synthase is complex V, so those statements are correct.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Respiration in Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollen viability",q:"In some members of which of the following pairs of families, pollen grains retain their viability for months after release?",o:["Rosaceae ; Leguminosae","Poaceae ; Rosaceae","Poaceae ; Leguminosae","Poaceae ; Solanaceae"],c:0,s:`Pollen of some Rosaceae, Leguminosae and Solanaceae stays viable for months.
In Poaceae (cereals) pollen loses viability within about 30 minutes.
So the pair is Rosaceae and Leguminosae.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription",q:"Identify the correct statement.",o:["Split gene arrangement is characteristic of prokaryotes","In capping, methyl guanosine triphosphate is added to the 3' end of hnRNA","RNA polymerase binds with Rho factor to terminate the process of transcription in bacteria","The coding strand in a transcription unit is copied to an mRNA"],c:2,s:`Split genes are a feature of eukaryotes, not prokaryotes.
Capping adds methyl guanosine triphosphate at the 5' end; the 3' end gets the poly-A tail.
The template (non-coding) strand is copied to mRNA, not the coding strand.
In bacteria, the Rho factor with RNA polymerase terminates transcription, so 3 is correct.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Floral formulae of families",q:`Match Column-I with Column-II
Column-I floral formulae: (a) %\u2642\u2640 K(5) C1+2+(2) A(9)+1 G1 (b) \u2295\u2642\u2640 K(5) C(5) A5 G(2) (c) \u2295\u2642\u2640 P(3+3) A3+3 G(3) (d) \u2295\u2642\u2640 K2+2 C4 A2+4 G(2)
Column-II: (i) Brassicaceae (ii) Liliaceae (iii) Fabaceae (iv) Solanaceae`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:1,s:`(a) Zygomorphic (%), 5 sepals, petals 1+2+(2) (standard, wings, keel), 9+1 stamens: Fabaceae (iii).
(b) Actinomorphic, 5 fused sepals and petals, 5 stamens on petals, bicarpellary: Solanaceae (iv).
(c) Perianth 3+3, 3+3 stamens, 3 fused carpels: Liliaceae (ii).
(d) 2+2 sepals, 4 petals, 2+4 stamens (tetradynamous): Brassicaceae (i).
Match: a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i.`,fig:{w:425,h:192,alt:"Table matching four floral formulae in List-I (a) to (d) with the families Brassicaceae, Liliaceae, Fabaceae and Solanaceae in List-II (i) to (iv)",d:[["l",0,0,0,186],["l",36,0,36,186],["l",262,0,262,186],["l",302,0,302,186],["l",420,0,420,186],["l",0,0,420,0],["l",0,26,420,26],["l",0,66,420,66],["l",0,106,420,106],["l",0,146,420,146],["l",0,186,420,186],["t",148,18,"List-I","m"],["t",360,18,"List-II","m"],["t",18,49,"(a)","m"],["t",282,49,"(i)","m"],["t",310,49,"Brassicaceae",""],["t",18,89,"(b)","m"],["t",282,89,"(ii)","m"],["t",310,89,"Liliaceae",""],["t",18,129,"(c)","m"],["t",282,129,"(iii)","m"],["t",310,129,"Fabaceae",""],["t",18,169,"(d)","m"],["t",282,169,"(iv)","m"],["t",310,169,"Solanaceae",""],["t",50,53,"%","m"],["t",66,50,"\u2642","m"],["t",63,60,"\u2640","m"],["t",78,53,"K",""],["t",87.5,53,"\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",110.3,53,"C",""],["t",119.8,53,"\u2081\u208A\u2082\u208A\u208D\u2082\u208E",""],["t",169,53,"A",""],["t",178.5,53,"\u208D\u2089\u208E\u208A\u2081",""],["t",214.5,53,"G",""],["l",214.5,56,223.5,56],["t",224,53,"\u2081",""],["t",50,93,"\u2295","m"],["t",66,90,"\u2642","m"],["t",63,100,"\u2640","m"],["t",78,93,"K",""],["t",87.5,93,"\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",110.3,93,"C",""],["t",119.8,93,"\u208D\u2085\u208E",""],["t",142.6,93,"A",""],["t",152.1,93,"\u2085",""],["t",161.7,93,"G",""],["l",161.7,96,170.7,96],["t",171.2,93,"\u208D\u2082\u208E",""],["p","M114.3 81 Q140.3 67 150.6 81"],["t",50,133,"\u2295","m"],["t",66,130,"\u2642","m"],["t",63,140,"\u2640","m"],["t",78,133,"P",""],["t",87.5,133,"\u208D\u2083\u208A\u2083\u208E",""],["t",123.5,133,"A",""],["t",133,133,"\u2083\u208A\u2083",""],["t",155.8,133,"G",""],["l",155.8,136,164.8,136],["t",165.3,133,"\u208D\u2083\u208E",""],["p","M81 121 Q113.5 106 131.5 121"],["t",50,173,"\u2295","m"],["t",66,170,"\u2642","m"],["t",63,180,"\u2640","m"],["t",78,173,"K",""],["t",87.5,173,"\u2082\u208A\u2082",""],["t",110.3,173,"C",""],["t",119.8,173,"\u2084",""],["t",129.39999999999998,173,"A",""],["t",138.89999999999998,173,"\u2082\u208B\u2084",""],["t",161.7,173,"G",""],["l",161.7,176,170.7,176],["t",171.2,173,"\u208D\u2082\u208E",""]]},sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2021-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Bonds in biomolecules",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Protein (b) Unsaturated fatty acid (c) Nucleic acid (d) Polysaccharide
List-II: (i) C = C double bonds (ii) Phosphodiester bonds (iii) Glycosidic bonds (iv) Peptide bonds`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],c:1,s:`Proteins are chains of amino acids joined by peptide bonds: a-iv.
Unsaturated fatty acids have one or more C=C double bonds: b-i.
Nucleotides in nucleic acids are joined by phosphodiester bonds: c-ii.
Sugars in a polysaccharide are joined by glycosidic bonds: d-iii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2021-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Cell cycle phases",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (a) S phase (b) G2 phase (c) Quiescent stage (d) G1 phase
List-II: (i) Proteins are synthesized (ii) Inactive phase (iii) Interval between mitosis and initiation of DNA replication (iv) DNA replication`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],c:3,s:`S phase is when DNA is replicated: a-iv.
G2 is when proteins and RNA needed for mitosis are made: b-i.
The quiescent stage (G\u2080) is an inactive stage: c-ii.
G1 is the gap between mitosis and the start of DNA replication: d-iii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2021-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Molecular diagnosis, probes",q:"Now a days it is possible to detect the mutated gene causing cancer by allowing radioactive probe to hybridise its complimentary DNA in a clone of cells, followed by its detection using autoradiography because :",o:["mutated gene does not appear on photographic film as the probe has complementarity with it","mutated gene partially appears on a photographic film","mutated gene completely and clearly appears on a photographic film","mutated gene does not appear on a photographic film as the probe has no complementarity with it"],c:3,s:`The radioactive probe is made to match the normal gene.
It hybridises (pairs) only with the normal complementary DNA, which then shows up on the film.
The mutated gene has a changed sequence, so the probe cannot pair with it and it does not appear on the film.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2021-q151",pn:151,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Endocrine function of kidney",q:"Erythropoietin hormone which stimulates R.B.C. formation is produced by:",o:["Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney","Alpha cells of pancreas","The cells of rostral adenohypophysis","The cells of bone marrow"],c:0,s:`When oxygen supply falls, the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney release erythropoietin.
It acts on bone marrow to make more red blood cells.
Alpha cells of the pancreas make glucagon, the anterior pituitary makes its own hormones, and bone marrow makes the cells but not the hormone.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2021-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach anatomy",q:"Which of the following characteristics is incorrect with respect to cockroach?",o:["10th abdominal segment in both sexes, bears a pair of anal cerci","A ring of gastric caeca is present at the junction of midgut and hind gut","Hypopharynx lies within the cavity enclosed by the mouth parts","In females, 7th - 9th sterna together form a genital pouch"],c:1,s:`Gastric caeca form a ring at the junction of foregut and midgut, not midgut and hindgut.
At the midgut-hindgut junction there are Malpighian tubules.
The other three statements are correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2021-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Digestive juices",q:"Succus entericus is referred to as:",o:["Chyme","Pancreatic juice","Intestinal juice","Gastric juice"],c:2,s:`Succus entericus is the intestinal juice secreted by the small intestine.
Chyme is the acidic food in the stomach, pancreatic juice comes from the pancreas and gastric juice from the stomach.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2021-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiotic prophase I",q:"Which stage of meiotic prophase shows terminalisation of chiasmata as its distinctive feature?",o:["Pachytene","Leptotene","Zygotene","Diakinesis"],c:3,s:`Chiasmata appear in diplotene and then move to the ends (terminalisation) in diakinesis.
Pachytene is crossing over, zygotene is pairing and leptotene is chromosome condensation.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2021-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Partial pressures of gases",q:"The partial pressures (in mm Hg) of oxygen (O\u2082) and carbon dioxide (CO\u2082) at alveoli (the site of diffusion) are:",o:["pO\u2082 = 159 and pCO\u2082 = 0.3","pO\u2082 = 104 and pCO\u2082 = 40","pO\u2082 = 40 and pCO\u2082 = 45","pO\u2082 = 95 and pCO\u2082 = 40"],c:1,s:`Alveolar air: pO\u2082 = 104 mm Hg, pCO\u2082 = 40 mm Hg.
159 and 0.3 are atmospheric values; 40 and 45 are deoxygenated blood; 95 and 40 are oxygenated blood.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2021-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Common names of animals",q:`Match the following:
List-I: (a) Physalia (b) Limulus (c) Ancylostoma (d) Pinctada
List-II: (i) Pearl oyster (ii) Portuguese Man of War (iii) Living fossil (iv) Hookworm`,o:["(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Physalia is the Portuguese man-of-war: a-ii.
Limulus (king crab) is a living fossil: b-iii.
Ancylostoma is the hookworm: c-iv.
Pinctada is the pearl oyster: d-i.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR steps",q:"During the process of gene amplification using PCR, if very high temperature is not maintained in the beginning, then which of the following steps of PCR will be affected first?",o:["Ligation","Annealing","Extension","Denaturation"],c:3,s:`The first step of PCR is denaturation at about 94 \xB0C, which separates the DNA strands.
Without the high temperature this step fails first.
Annealing (50-60 \xB0C) comes next, and ligation is not a PCR step.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2021-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Microbes and products",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Aspergillus niger (b) Acetobacter aceti (c) Clostridium butylicum (d) Lactobacillus
List-II: (i) Acetic Acid (ii) Lactic Acid (iii) Citric Acid (iv) Butyric Acid`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],c:1,s:`Aspergillus niger gives citric acid: a-iii.
Acetobacter aceti gives acetic acid: b-i.
Clostridium butylicum gives butyric acid: c-iv.
Lactobacillus gives lactic acid: d-ii.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2021-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle types",q:"Which of the following statements wrongly represents the nature of smooth muscle?",o:["These muscles are present in the wall of blood vessels","These muscle have no striations","They are involuntary muscles","Communication among the cells is performed by intercalated discs"],c:3,s:`Smooth muscle is non-striated, involuntary and lines vessel walls, gut and uterus.
Intercalated discs occur only in cardiac muscle, so statement 4 is wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2021-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscular disorders",q:"Chronic auto immune disorder affecting neuro muscular junction leading to fatigue, weakening and paralysis of skeletal muscle is called as :",o:["Gout","Arthritis","Muscular dystrophy","Myasthenia gravis"],c:3,s:`Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease at the neuromuscular junction that causes fatigue and weakness of skeletal muscle.
Gout is uric acid crystals in joints, arthritis is joint inflammation and muscular dystrophy is a genetic degeneration of muscle.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2021-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood groups",q:`Persons with 'AB' blood group are called as "Universal recipients". This is due to :`,o:["Absence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B, in plasma","Absence of antigens A and B on the surface of RBCs","Absence of antigens A and B in plasma","Presence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B, on RBCs"],c:0,s:`AB persons have both antigens A and B on their red cells.
Their plasma has neither anti-A nor anti-B, so no antibody attacks donor cells of any group.
That is why they are universal recipients.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2021-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"S phase",q:"The fruit fly has 8 chromosomes (2n) in each cell. During interphase of Mitosis if the number of chromosomes at G1 phase is 8, what would be the number of chromosomes after S phase?",o:["32","8","16","4"],c:1,s:`In S phase the DNA is copied, so the DNA amount doubles.
But the two copies stay joined at the centromere as sister chromatids, and are still counted as one chromosome each.
So the number of chromosomes stays 8.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2021-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Fertilisation",q:"Receptors for sperm binding in mammals are present on :",o:["Zona pellucida","Corona radiata","Vitelline membrane","Perivitelline space"],c:0,s:`The zona pellucida carries the receptors (ZP3) that bind the sperm in mammals.
Corona radiata is a layer of cells and the perivitelline space is a gap, so neither holds receptors.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2021-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Classification of insects",q:"Which one of the following belongs to the family Muscidae?",o:["House fly","Fire fly","Grasshopper","Cockroach"],c:0,s:`The house fly (Musca) is in family Muscidae.
Fire fly is Lampyridae, grasshopper is Acrididae and cockroach is Blattidae.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Plant products",q:"Identify the incorrect pair",o:["Drugs \u2013 Ricin","Alkaloids \u2013 Codeine","Toxin \u2013 Abrin","Lectins \u2013 Concanavalin A"],c:0,s:`Ricin is a toxin (from Ricinus), not a drug, so pair 1 is wrong.
Codeine is an alkaloid, abrin is a toxin and concanavalin A is a lectin, so the other pairs are right.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2021-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Sexually transmitted diseases",q:`Veneral diseases can spread through :
(a) Using sterile needles (b) Transfusion of blood from infected person (c) Infected mother to foetus (d) Kissing (e) Inheritance`,o:["(a) and (c) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(b), (c) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only"],c:3,s:`Sexually transmitted infections spread by sharing infected needles, by blood transfusion and from an infected mother to her foetus.
(a) says sterile needles, which do not spread infection.
Kissing and inheritance do not spread them.
So only (b) and (c).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2021-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Endomembrane system",q:"The organelles that are included in the endomembrane system are",o:["Golgi complex, Endoplasmic reticulum, Mitochondria and Lysosomes","Endoplasmic reticulum, Mitochondria, Ribosomes and Lysosomes","Endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, Lysosomes and Vacuoles","Golgi complex, Mitochondria, Ribosomes and Lysosomes"],c:2,s:`The endomembrane system is ER, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuoles, whose functions are coordinated.
Mitochondria are semi-autonomous and ribosomes have no membrane, so they are not included.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2021-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Molecular diagnosis",q:"For effective treatment of the disease, early diagnosis and understanding its pathophysiology is very important. Which of the following molecular diagnostic techniques is very useful for early detection?",o:["Hybridization Technique","Western Blotting Technique","Southern Blotting Technique","ELISA Technique"],a:"3/4*",s:`ELISA detects antigens or antibodies of a pathogen, and can catch an infection early.
Southern blotting detects a specific DNA sequence, so pathogen DNA can be found even before antibodies form.
Hybridisation with probes is used to find mutated genes, and Western blotting detects a specific protein.
So both option 3 and option 4 can be argued as correct.
Note: Printed key is "3/4*" (two options); not a plain 1-4.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2021-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Insulin",q:`With regard to insulin choose correct options.
(a) C-peptide is not present in mature insulin.
(b) The insulin produced by rDNA technology has Cpeptide.
(c) The pro-insulin has C-peptide
(d) A-peptide and B-peptide of insulin are interconnected by disulphide bridges.`,o:["(a) and (d) only","(b) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only","(a), (c) and (d) only"],c:3,s:`Insulin is made as pro-insulin with chains A and B plus a C-peptide: (c) is true.
The C-peptide is cut out, so mature insulin has none: (a) true.
Recombinant insulin is the mature form, so it has no C-peptide: (b) false.
Chains A and B are joined by disulphide bridges: (d) true.
So (a), (c) and (d).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2021-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"A specific recognition sequence identified by endonucleases to make cuts at specific positions within the DNA is:",o:["Poly(A) tail sequences","Degenerate primer sequence","Okazaki sequences","Palindromic Nucleotide sequences"],c:3,s:`A restriction endonuclease recognises a palindromic sequence (reads the same on both strands in the 5' to 3' direction) and cuts both strands there.
Poly(A) tails, degenerate primers and Okazaki fragments are unrelated.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2021-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription in bacteria",q:'Which is the "Only enzyme" that has "Capability" to catalyse Initiation, Elongation and Termination in the process of transcription in prokaryotes?',o:["DNase","DNA dependent DNA polymerase","DNA dependent RNA polymerase","DNA Ligase"],c:2,s:`In bacteria one DNA-dependent RNA polymerase does initiation (with the sigma factor), elongation and termination (with rho).
DNase breaks DNA, DNA polymerase replicates DNA and ligase joins DNA fragments.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Birds",q:"Which one of the following organisms bears hollow and pneumatic long bones ?",o:["Ornithorhynchus","Neophron","Hemidactylus","Macropus"],c:1,s:`Hollow, air-filled (pneumatic) bones are a feature of birds (class Aves).
Neophron (vulture) is a bird; Ornithorhynchus and Macropus are mammals and Hemidactylus is a reptile.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Features of animal phyla",q:`Read the following statements
(a) Metagenesis is observed in Helminths.
(b) Echinoderms are triploblastic and coelomate animals.
(c) Round worms have organ-system level of body organization.
(d) Comb plates present in ctenophores help in digestion.
(e) Water vascular system is characteristic of Echinoderms.`,o:["(b), (c) and (e) are correct","(c), (d) and (e) are correct","(a), (b) and (c) are correct","(a), (d) and (e) are correct"],c:0,s:`(a) is wrong: metagenesis is seen in cnidarians.
(b) is right: echinoderms are triploblastic with a true coelom.
(c) is right: roundworms have organ-system organisation.
(d) is wrong: comb plates are for locomotion.
(e) is right: the water vascular system is typical of echinoderms.
So (b), (c) and (e).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Contraceptive methods",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (a) Vaults (b) IUDs (c) Vasectomy (d) Tubectomy
List-II: (i) Entry of sperm through Cervix is blocked (ii) Removal of Vas deferens (iii) Phagocytosis of sperms within the Uterus (iv) Removal of fallopian tube`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iiii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:2,s:`Vaults are barriers over the cervix, blocking sperm entry: a-i.
IUDs increase phagocytosis of sperm in the uterus: b-iii.
Vasectomy removes (or ties) part of the vas deferens: c-ii.
Tubectomy removes (or ties) part of the fallopian tube: d-iv.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2021-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Sphincter of Oddi",q:"Sphincter of oddi is present at:",o:["Junction of jejunum and duodenum","Ileo-caecal junction","Junction of hepato-pancreatic duct and duodenum","Gastro-oesophageal junction"],c:2,s:`The bile duct and pancreatic duct join as the hepato-pancreatic duct, which opens into the duodenum.
The sphincter of Oddi guards this opening.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2021-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Chargaff's rule",q:"If Adenine makes 30% of the DNA molecule, what will be the percentage of Thymine, Guanine and Cytosine in it?",o:["T : 20 ; G : 25 ; C : 25","T : 20 ; G : 30 ; C : 20","T : 20 ; G : 20 ; C : 30","T : 30 ; G : 20 ; C : 20"],c:3,s:`Chargaff's rule: in double-stranded DNA, A = T and G = C.
A = 30%, so T = 30%.
A + T = 60%, so G + C = 100 \u2013 60 = 40%.
G = C = 20% each.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Features of phyla",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Metamerism (b) Canal System (c) Comb plates (d) Cnidoblasts
List-II: (i) Coelenterata (ii) Ctenophora (iii) Annelida (iv) Porifera`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Metamerism (segmentation) is a feature of Annelida: a-iii.
A canal system is found in sponges (Porifera): b-iv.
Comb plates belong to Ctenophora: c-ii.
Cnidoblasts are stinging cells of Coelenterata: d-i.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2021-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood clotting",q:"Which enzyme is responsible for the conversion of inactive fibrinogens to fibrins?",o:["Thrombokinase","Thrombin","Renin","Epinephrine"],c:1,s:`Thrombokinase (enzyme complex) converts prothrombin to thrombin.
Thrombin then converts fibrinogen to fibrin threads, which form the clot.
Renin and epinephrine have other roles.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2021-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Biofortification",q:"Which of the following is not an objective of Biofortification in crops?",o:["Improve micronutrient and mineral content","Improve protein content","Improve resistance to diseases","Improve vitamin content"],c:2,s:`Biofortification breeds crops for better nutrition: more protein, vitamins, minerals and micronutrients.
Disease resistance is a different breeding goal, so it is not an objective of biofortification.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2021-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Ozone and Dobson units",q:"Dobson units are used to measure thickness of:",o:["Troposphere","CFCs","Stratosphere","Ozone"],c:3,s:`The thickness of the ozone layer in a column of air is measured in Dobson units.
One Dobson unit is the thickness a 0.01 mm layer of pure ozone would have at standard pressure and temperature.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2021-q181",pn:181,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"IUDs",q:"Which one of the following is an example of Hormone releasing IUD?",o:["Multiload 375","CuT","LNG 20","Cu 7"],c:2,s:`LNG-20 releases the hormone levonorgestrel.
Multiload 375, CuT and Cu7 are copper-releasing IUDs.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2021-q182",pn:182,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Centriole duplication",q:"The centriole undergoes duplication during:",o:["G2 phase","S-phase","Prophase","Metaphase"],c:1,s:`In animal cells the centriole is duplicated in the cytoplasm during S phase, along with DNA replication.
The two centrosomes then separate in prophase.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2021-q183",pn:183,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Types of RNA",q:"Which of the following RNAs is not required for the synthesis of protein?",o:["siRNA","mRNA","tRNA","rRNA"],c:0,s:`Protein synthesis needs mRNA (the message), tRNA (carries amino acids) and rRNA (part of the ribosome).
siRNA is small interfering RNA that silences genes; it is not needed for making proteins.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q184",pn:184,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sickle cell anaemia",q:"In a cross between a male and female, both heterozygous for sickle cell anaemia gene, what percentage of the progeny will be diseased?",o:["100%","50%","75%","25%"],c:3,s:`Both parents are Aa (A normal, a sickle).
Aa \xD7 Aa gives AA : Aa : aa = 1 : 2 : 1.
Only aa is diseased, which is 1 in 4 = 25%.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2021-q185",pn:185,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Oxygen binding to haemoglobin",q:"Select the favourable conditions required for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin at the alveoli.",o:["Low pO\u2082, low pCO\u2082, more H\u207A, higher temperature","High pO\u2082, low pCO\u2082, less H\u207A, lower temperature","Low pO\u2082, high pCO\u2082, more H\u207A, higher temperature","High pO\u2082, high pCO\u2082, less H\u207A, higher temperature"],c:1,s:`At the alveoli pO\u2082 is high and pCO\u2082 is low.
Less H\u207A (higher pH) and lower temperature also favour binding of O\u2082 to haemoglobin.
That is high pO\u2082, low pCO\u2082, less H\u207A and lower temperature.
The reverse set of conditions occurs at tissues and releases O\u2082.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2021-q186",pn:186,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Adaptations",q:`Match List-I with List - II
List-I: (a) Allen's Rule (b) Physiological adaptation (c) Behavioural adaptation (d) Biochemical adaptation
List-II: (i) Kangaroo rat (ii) Desert lizard (iii) Marine fish at depth (iv) Polar seal`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],c:3,s:`Allen's rule: mammals in cold regions have shorter limbs and ears, as in the polar seal: a-iv.
The kangaroo rat copes with desert by physiology (concentrated urine): b-i.
The desert lizard basks and burrows, a behavioural adaptation: c-ii.
Deep-sea fish have biochemical adaptations to depth: d-iii.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2021-q187",pn:187,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Evolution",st:"Types of evolution",q:`Match List - I with List - II
List-I: (a) Adaptive radiation (b) Convergent evolution (c) Divergent evolution (d) Evolution by anthropogenic action
List-II: (i) Selection of resistant varieties due to excessive use of herbicides and pesticides (ii) Bones of forelimbs in Man and Whale (iii) Wings of Butterfly and Bird (iv) Darwin Finches`,o:["(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],c:1,s:`Adaptive radiation from one ancestor: Darwin's finches, a-iv.
Convergent evolution gives analogous organs: wings of butterfly and bird, b-iii.
Divergent evolution gives homologous organs: forelimb bones of man and whale, c-ii.
Human-caused evolution: resistant varieties after heavy herbicide and pesticide use, d-i.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2021-q188",pn:188,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Parturition",q:"Which of these is not an important component of initiation of parturition in humans ?",o:["Release of Prolactin","Increase in estrogen and progesterone ratio","Synthesis of prostaglandins","Release of Oxytocin"],c:0,s:`Birth is started by a rising estrogen to progesterone ratio, prostaglandins and oxytocin, which make the uterus contract.
Prolactin is for milk production and does not start parturition.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2021-q189",pn:189,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Histones",q:"Which one of the following statements about Histones is wrong?",o:["Histones carry positive charge in the side chain","Histones are organized to form a unit of 8 molecules","The pH of histones is slightly acidic","Histones are rich in amino acids - Lysine and Arginine"],c:2,s:`Histones are rich in lysine and arginine, which are basic amino acids with positively charged side chains.
Eight histone molecules form an octamer.
Because they are basic, they are not slightly acidic, so statement 3 is wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q190",pn:190,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle contraction",q:`During muscular contraction which of the following events occur?
(a) 'H' zone disappears (b) 'A' band widens (c) 'I' band reduces in width (d) Myosine hydrolyzes ATP, releasing the ADP and Pi. (e) Z-lines attached to actins are pulled inwards.`,o:["(b), (d), (e), (a) only","(a), (c), (d), (e) only","(a), (b), (c), (d) only","(b), (c), (d), (e) only"],c:1,s:`Myosin heads hydrolyse ATP and release ADP and Pi as they pull actin: (d) occurs.
Actin filaments slide inward, pulling the Z-lines closer: (e) occurs.
The H zone narrows and disappears, and the I band gets shorter: (a) and (c) occur.
The A band keeps the same length, so (b) is wrong.
So (a), (c), (d), (e).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2021-q191",pn:191,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Bones and joints",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Scapula (b) Cranium (c) Sternum (d) Vertebral
List-II: (i) Cartilaginous joints (ii) Flat bone (iii) Fibrous joints (iv) Triangular flat bone`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:0,s:`The scapula is a triangular flat bone: a-iv.
Skull bones of the cranium are joined by fibrous joints (sutures): b-iii.
The sternum is a flat bone: c-ii.
Vertebrae are joined by cartilaginous joints: d-i.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2021-q192",pn:192,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Altitude sickness",q:`Assertion (A): A person goes to high altitude and experiences 'altitude sickness' with symptoms like breathing difficulty and heart palpitations.
Reason (R): Due to low atmospheric pressure at high altitude, the body does not get sufficient oxygen. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false"],c:1,s:`At high altitude the atmospheric pressure, and so pO\u2082, is low, so the body gets less oxygen.
This causes breathing difficulty, fatigue and palpitations.
Both are true and R explains A.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2021-q193",pn:193,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Gene therapy for ADA deficiency",q:"The Adenosine deaminase deficiency results into",o:["Addison's disease","Dysfunction of Immune system","Parkinson's disease","Digestive disorder"],c:1,s:`Adenosine deaminase (ADA) is needed for the immune system to work (T-lymphocytes).
Its lack causes dysfunction of the immune system (SCID); gene therapy was first tried for it.
Addison's disease is adrenal cortex hyposecretion and Parkinson's disease affects the nervous system.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2021-q194",pn:194,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"MOET in cattle",q:"Which of the following is not a step in Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer Technology (MOET)?",o:["Fertilized eggs are transferred to surrogate mothers at 8-32 cell stage","Cow is administered hormone having LH like activity for super ovulation","Cow yields about 6-8 eggs at a time","Cow is fertilized by artificial insemination"],c:1,s:`In MOET the cow is given a hormone with FSH-like activity (not LH-like) to cause superovulation.
She yields 6-8 eggs, is mated or artificially inseminated, and the 8-32 cell embryos are moved to surrogate mothers.
So statement 2 is the wrong step.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2021-q195",pn:195,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Diseases and causative organisms",q:`Match List-I with List-II
List-I: (a) Filariasis (b) Amoebiasis (c) Pneumonia (d) Ringworm
List-II: (i) Haemophilus influenzae (ii) Trichophyton (iii) Wuchereria bancrofti (iv) Entamoeba histolytica`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],c:2,s:`Filariasis is caused by the worm Wuchereria bancrofti: a-iii.
Amoebiasis is caused by Entamoeba histolytica: b-iv.
Pneumonia can be caused by Haemophilus influenzae: c-i.
Ringworm is a fungal disease caused by Trichophyton: d-ii.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2021-q196",pn:196,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Lipids",q:`Following are the statements with reference to 'lipids'.
(a) Lipids having only single bonds are called unsaturated fatty acids
(b) Lecithin is a phospholipid.
(c) Trihydroxy propane is glycerol.
(d) Palmitic acid has 20 carbon atoms including carboxyl carbon.`,o:["(b) and (e) only","(a) and (b) only","(c) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only"],c:3,s:`(a) is wrong: fatty acids with only single bonds are saturated.
(b) is right: lecithin is a phospholipid.
(c) is right: glycerol is trihydroxy propane.
(d) is wrong: palmitic acid has 16 carbons (arachidonic acid has 20).
So (b) and (c) only.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2021-q197",pn:197,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Earthworm",q:`Following are the statements about prostomium of earthworm.
(a) It serves as a covering for mouth.
(b) It helps to open cracks in the soil into which it can crawl.
(c) It is one of the sensory structures.
(d) It is the first body segment.`,o:["(b) and (c) are correct","(a), (b) and (c) are correct","(a), (b) and (d) are correct","(a), (b), (c) and (d) are correct"],c:1,s:`The prostomium is a lobe over the mouth: (a) right.
It acts as a wedge to open soil cracks: (b) right.
It has receptors, so it is sensory: (c) right.
It is not a segment; the first segment is the peristomium: (d) wrong.
So (a), (b) and (c).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2021-q198",pn:198,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Hormones of pregnancy",q:"Which of the following secretes the hormone, relaxin, during the later phase of pregnancy?",o:["Uterus","Graafian follicle","Corpus luteum","Foetus"],c:2,s:`Relaxin is secreted by the ovary in late pregnancy.
The structure that does this is the corpus luteum (the ruptured follicle that becomes an endocrine gland).
No Graafian follicle develops in pregnancy, and the uterus and foetus do not make relaxin.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2021-q199",pn:199,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Genetic code",q:`Statement I: The condon 'AUG' codes for methionine and phenylalanine.
Statement II: 'AAA' and 'AAG' both codons code for the amino acid lysine.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct but Statement II is false"],c:0,s:`AUG codes only for methionine (and also starts translation); phenylalanine is UUU/UUC. Statement I is false.
AAA and AAG both code for lysine (the code is degenerate). Statement II is true.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2021-q200",pn:200,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cell junctions",q:"Identify the types of cell junctions that help to stop the leakage of the substances across a tissue and facilitation of communication with neighbouring cells via rapid transfer of ions and molecules.",o:["Adhering junctions and Gap junctions, respectively","Gap junctions and Adhering junctions, respectively","Tight junctions and Gap junctions, respectively","Adhering junctions and Tight junctions, respectively."],c:2,s:`Tight junctions seal cells together so substances cannot leak across a tissue.
Gap junctions link the cytoplasm of neighbouring cells so ions and small molecules pass quickly.
Adhering junctions only hold cells together.
So tight junctions, then gap junctions.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"}]})();
